// Authored variants: the same fact asked from a different angle (application, scenario, prediction, NOT/EXCEPT)
const QB_VARIANTS_2 = [
  {ofTag:'Equilibrium',of:'If Q < Keq, the reaction will proceed _____',kind:'prediction',q:'For N₂(g) + 3H₂(g) ⇌ 2NH₃(g), Keq = 0.50 at a certain temperature. A flask at that temperature holds a mixture with Q = 0.050. What happens as the system approaches equilibrium?',choices:['Keq decreases until it equals Q','The net reaction converts NH₃ back to N₂ and H₂','The net reaction forms more NH₃','No net reaction occurs'],ans:2,exp:'Because Q (0.050) is smaller than Keq (0.50), the mixture holds too little product relative to equilibrium, so the net reaction runs **forward, toward products**, raising Q until Q = Keq. Keq changes only with temperature, never to match Q; net consumption of NH₃ would be the response if Q were greater than Keq.'},
  {ofTag:'Equilibrium',of:'Le Châtelier\'s principle states that if a stress is applied to a system at equilibrium, the system _____',kind:'application',q:'The synthesis 2SO₂(g) + O₂(g) ⇌ 2SO₃(g) is exothermic. Which change will increase the equilibrium yield of SO₃?',choices:['Removing some O₂','Raising the temperature','Adding a catalyst','Decreasing the container volume'],ans:3,exp:'Compressing the system raises the pressure, and the equilibrium **shifts toward the side with fewer moles of gas** (3 mol of reactant gas → 2 mol of product gas) to relieve that stress. Heating an exothermic reaction shifts it toward reactants, removing O₂ pulls it left, and a catalyst speeds both directions equally without changing the equilibrium position.'},
  {ofTag:'Equilibrium',of:'Pure solids and liquids are excluded from the reaction quotient and equilibrium constant because _____',kind:'prediction',q:'CaCO₃(s) ⇌ CaO(s) + CO₂(g) is at equilibrium in a sealed vessel at constant temperature. A student adds more solid CaCO₃. What happens to the partial pressure of CO₂?',choices:['It stays the same, because Keq = P(CO₂) only','It doubles, because more solid is present','It increases, because more reactant is present','It decreases, because the reaction shifts left'],ans:0,exp:'For this reaction **Keq = P(CO₂)**: pure solids have an activity of 1 and drop out of the expression, so adding CaCO₃(s) creates no stress and P(CO₂) is unchanged. The "more reactant pushes the reaction right" reasoning works for gases and solutes, not for pure solids or liquids.'},
  {ofTag:'Thermochemistry',of:'If ΔG < 0, the reaction will be _____',kind:'prediction',q:'A reaction has ΔH = +40 kJ/mol and ΔS = +100 J/(mol·K). Assuming both are independent of temperature, above what temperature does the reaction become spontaneous?',choices:['4000 K','400 K','0.4 K','40 K'],ans:1,exp:'Spontaneity requires ΔG = ΔH − TΔS < 0, so T > ΔH/ΔS = 40,000 J/mol ÷ 100 J/(mol·K) = **400 K**. Forgetting to convert kJ to J gives the 0.4 K trap; with ΔH and ΔS both positive, the reaction is entropy-driven and becomes spontaneous only at high temperature.'},
  {ofTag:'Thermochemistry',of:'If ΔG > 0, the reaction will be _____',kind:'prediction',q:'A reaction has ΔH < 0 and ΔS < 0. Under which conditions will it be non-spontaneous (ΔG > 0)?',choices:['At all temperatures','At no temperature','At high temperatures only','At low temperatures only'],ans:2,exp:'With ΔG = ΔH − TΔS, a negative ΔS makes the −TΔS term positive, and that term grows with T; once T exceeds ΔH/ΔS it outweighs the favorable ΔH and **ΔG becomes positive at high temperature**. At low temperature the negative ΔH dominates, so the reaction is enthalpy-driven and spontaneous, as when water freezes below 0 °C.'},
  {ofTag:'Thermochemistry',of:'If ΔG = 0, the reaction will be _____',kind:'application',q:'Ice and liquid water coexist at 0 °C and 1 atm, with neither phase growing at the expense of the other. Which statement describes melting under these conditions?',choices:['ΔH = 0, so no heat is exchanged','ΔG < 0, so melting is favored','ΔG > 0, so freezing is favored','ΔG = 0, so neither direction is favored'],ans:3,exp:'At the normal melting point the two phases are **at equilibrium, so ΔG = 0** and ΔH = TΔS. Melting still absorbs heat (ΔH_fus ≈ +6 kJ/mol); the temperature stays constant because that energy goes into overcoming intermolecular attractions, not because ΔH is zero.'},
  {ofTag:'Thermochemistry',of:'Phase changes from solid → liquid → gas are _____',kind:'except',q:'Each of the following processes absorbs heat from its surroundings EXCEPT:',choices:['Water vapor condensing on a cold glass','Dry ice subliming in air','Sweat evaporating from skin','Ice melting in a drink'],ans:0,exp:'**Condensation** (gas → liquid) releases heat as intermolecular attractions form, so it is exothermic. Melting, sublimation and evaporation all move toward a less ordered phase and are endothermic, which is why evaporating sweat cools the body.'},
  {ofTag:'Chemical Kinetics',of:'A reaction whose rate does not depend on reactant concentration (rate = k) is _____',kind:'application',q:'Ethanol is metabolized by alcohol dehydrogenase, which is saturated at typical blood alcohol levels, so elimination follows zero-order kinetics. If a person\'s blood alcohol concentration were doubled, the elimination rate would:',choices:['quadruple','stay the same','double','halve'],ans:1,exp:'In a **zeroth-order** process, rate = k, independent of concentration, so doubling the substrate leaves the rate unchanged; the body clears a roughly constant amount of alcohol per hour. Doubling would be expected for first-order kinetics, which applies only when the enzyme is far from saturation.'},
  {ofTag:'General Chemistry',of:'_____ is the exponent to which a reactant\'s concentration is raised in the rate law',kind:'prediction',q:'A reaction has the rate law rate = k[A]²[B]. If [A] is tripled and [B] is halved, by what factor does the rate change?',choices:['9','3','4.5','1.5'],ans:2,exp:'Each concentration factor is raised to its **reaction order**: (3)² × (½)¹ = 9 × 0.5 = **4.5**. Treating A as first order gives 1.5, and ignoring the change in B gives 9.'},
  {ofTag:'Acids and Bases: Buffers and Titrations',of:'When choosing a titration indicator, you want one that changes color in a range close to the _____',kind:'application',q:'A student titrates acetic acid (pKa 4.76) with NaOH; the equivalence point is near pH 8.7. Which indicator is the best choice?',choices:['Bromocresol green (changes at pH 3.8–5.4)','Methyl orange (changes at pH 3.1–4.4)','Methyl red (changes at pH 4.4–6.2)','Phenolphthalein (changes at pH 8.2–10.0)'],ans:3,exp:'An indicator should change color in a range that brackets the **equivalence point**, here about pH 8.7, which falls in the range of **phenolphthalein**. Indicators that change near pH 4–5 would signal an end point in the buffer region, near the half-equivalence point, long before the acid is fully neutralized.'},
  {ofTag:'Acids and Bases: Buffers and Titrations',of:'In which titration does the equivalence point fall above pH 7, with a distinct buffering region centered on the half-equivalence point (pH = pKa)?',kind:'prediction',q:'Formic acid (pKa 3.75) is titrated with NaOH. What is the pH at the equivalence point?',choices:['Above 7, because formate is a weak base','Exactly 7, because the acid is fully neutralized','Below 7, because unreacted formic acid remains','Equal to 3.75, because pH = pKa at this point'],ans:0,exp:'At equivalence all HCOOH has become **HCOO⁻, the conjugate base of a weak acid**, which takes protons from water and produces OH⁻, so the pH is above 7. pH = pKa holds at the half-equivalence point, not at equivalence, and a pH of exactly 7 occurs only in strong acid–strong base titrations.'},
  {ofTag:'Acids and Bases: Buffers and Titrations',of:'In which titration does the equivalence point fall below pH 7, with the salt formed acting as a weak acid?',kind:'scenario',q:'Aqueous ammonia is titrated with HCl. At the equivalence point the solution contains mainly NH₄Cl. What is its pH?',choices:['Above 7, because excess NH₃ remains','Below 7, because NH₄⁺ is a weak acid','Exactly 7, because the salt is neutral','Above 7, because Cl⁻ is a weak base'],ans:1,exp:'At equivalence the base has been converted to **NH₄⁺, a weak conjugate acid**, which donates protons to water and makes the solution acidic. Cl⁻ is the negligibly weak conjugate base of a strong acid and does not affect pH, and no excess NH₃ remains at the equivalence point.'},
  {ofTag:'Acids and Bases: Buffers and Titrations',of:'At the half-equivalence point (midpoint) of a titration, pH = _____',kind:'scenario',q:'A student titrates an unknown monoprotic weak acid with NaOH. The equivalence point is reached at 25.0 mL of base, and the pH after 12.5 mL of base was 5.2. What is the pKa of the acid?',choices:['10.4','2.6','5.2','8.8'],ans:2,exp:'12.5 mL is half the equivalence volume, so it marks the **half-equivalence point**, where [HA] = [A⁻]. By Henderson–Hasselbalch, pH = pKa + log(1) = pKa, so pKa = 5.2. Halving the pH (2.6) confuses half the volume with half the pH.'},
  {ofTag:'Acids and Bases: Buffers and Titrations',of:'Buffering capacity is a measure of the ability of a buffer to _____',kind:'prediction',q:'Buffer X is 0.10 M acetic acid with 0.10 M sodium acetate; Buffer Y is 1.0 M acetic acid with 1.0 M sodium acetate. Which statement is correct?',choices:['Both have the same pH and the same buffering capacity','X resists added base better because it is more dilute','Y has a lower pH and resists added acid better','Both have the same pH, but Y resists added acid better'],ans:3,exp:'pH depends on the **ratio** [A⁻]/[HA], which is 1 in both, so both have pH = pKa ≈ 4.76. **Buffering capacity** depends on the absolute amounts of weak acid and conjugate base, so the tenfold more concentrated Buffer Y can absorb about ten times as much added acid or base before its pH shifts appreciably.'},
  {ofTag:'Acids and Bases: pH and pKa',of:'[OH⁻] = _____',kind:'application',q:'Assuming 25 °C, what is the approximate [OH⁻] of a solution with pH 7.4?',choices:['2.5 × 10⁻⁷ M','6.6 × 10⁻⁷ M','4.0 × 10⁻⁸ M','1.0 × 10⁻⁷ M'],ans:0,exp:'pOH = 14 − 7.4 = 6.6, so [OH⁻] = 10^(−6.6) = 10^(0.4) × 10⁻⁷ ≈ **2.5 × 10⁻⁷ M**. The value 4.0 × 10⁻⁸ M is [H⁺] (10^−7.4), a common slip when the pOH step is skipped.'},
  {ofTag:'Reactions & Mechanisms',of:'A lower pKa means a _____ acid',kind:'application',q:'Compound X has pKa 4.2 and compound Y has pKa 9.8. Which statement is correct?',choices:['X and Y are equally strong acids in water','X is the stronger acid, and Y⁻ is the stronger conjugate base','Y is the stronger acid, and X⁻ is the stronger conjugate base','X is the stronger acid, and X⁻ is the stronger conjugate base'],ans:1,exp:'A **lower pKa means a larger Ka and a stronger acid**, so X is stronger by about 5.6 log units. Acid strength and conjugate-base strength are inversely related (Ka × Kb = Kw), so the weaker acid Y has the stronger conjugate base.'},
  {ofTag:'Carbonyl Chemistry',of:'The acidity of a carboxylic acid can be enhanced by substituents that are electron-_____',kind:'prediction',q:'Which of the following carboxylic acids has the lowest pKa?',choices:['Acetic acid','Chloroacetic acid','Trichloroacetic acid','Propanoic acid'],ans:2,exp:'Each chlorine is **electron-withdrawing** and stabilizes the carboxylate anion by induction, so trichloroacetic acid (pKa ≈ 0.7) is far more acidic than chloroacetic acid (≈ 2.9) or acetic acid (≈ 4.8). The extra alkyl carbon of propanoic acid is weakly electron-donating, making it slightly less acidic than acetic acid.'},
  {ofTag:'Solutions: Solubility',of:'The common ion effect causes solubility to _____ when like ions are added',kind:'prediction',q:'AgCl is dissolved to saturation in pure water and, separately, in 0.10 M NaCl. Compared with pure water, the molar solubility of AgCl in the NaCl solution is:',choices:['much higher','the same, because Ksp is constant','zero, because no AgCl can dissolve','much lower'],ans:3,exp:'The added Cl⁻ is a **common ion**: with [Cl⁻] fixed near 0.10 M, [Ag⁺] can rise only to Ksp/0.10 ≈ 1.8 × 10⁻⁹ M, versus about 1.3 × 10⁻⁵ M in pure water. Ksp itself is unchanged; what changes is how much solid can dissolve before Qsp reaches Ksp.'},
  {ofTag:'Solutions: Solubility',of:'If Qsp is _____ Ksp, the solution is supersaturated, so a precipitate will form',kind:'prediction',q:'Equal volumes of 2.0 × 10⁻⁴ M AgNO₃ and 2.0 × 10⁻⁴ M NaCl are mixed. Ksp(AgCl) = 1.8 × 10⁻¹⁰. What happens?',choices:['A precipitate forms, because Qsp > Ksp','No precipitate forms, because NaNO₃ is soluble','No precipitate forms, because Qsp < Ksp','The solution is exactly saturated, with Qsp = Ksp'],ans:0,exp:'Mixing equal volumes halves each concentration to 1.0 × 10⁻⁴ M, so Qsp = (1.0 × 10⁻⁴)² = 1.0 × 10⁻⁸, which **exceeds Ksp**; the solution is supersaturated and AgCl precipitates. Always account for dilution on mixing before comparing Qsp with Ksp.'},
  {ofTag:'Solutions',of:'Dissolving a nonvolatile solute in a liquid solvent causes the vapor pressure above the liquid to _____',kind:'prediction',q:'Glucose is dissolved in water. Compared with pure water at the same temperature, the solution has:',choices:['a higher vapor pressure and a lower boiling point','a lower vapor pressure and a higher boiling point','a lower vapor pressure and a lower boiling point','the same vapor pressure and a higher boiling point'],ans:1,exp:'A nonvolatile solute lowers the mole fraction of water, so by Raoult\'s law the **vapor pressure decreases**; the solution must then be heated further before its vapor pressure reaches atmospheric pressure, so the **boiling point rises**. Vapor-pressure lowering and boiling-point elevation always occur together.'},
  {ofTag:'Solutions',of:'_____ are physical properties of solutions that depend on the concentration of dissolved particles but not on their chemical identity',kind:'prediction',q:'Which 0.10 m aqueous solution has the lowest freezing point?',choices:['Acetic acid','NaCl','CaCl₂','Glucose'],ans:2,exp:'Freezing-point depression is a **colligative property**, ΔTf = i·Kf·m, so the solute that releases the most particles wins: CaCl₂ gives i ≈ 3, NaCl i ≈ 2, weakly ionized acetic acid only slightly above 1, and glucose i = 1. Particle count, not chemical identity, determines the effect.'},
  {ofTag:'Solutions',of:'_____ is the concentration of all dissolved particles (osmoles) per liter of solution',kind:'application',q:'Normal saline is 0.15 M NaCl. Assuming complete dissociation, what is its osmolarity?',choices:['0.45 osmol/L','0.075 osmol/L','0.15 osmol/L','0.30 osmol/L'],ans:3,exp:'Each NaCl yields two particles (Na⁺ and Cl⁻), so **osmolarity = i × M = 2 × 0.15 = 0.30 osmol/L**, about 300 mOsm/L, close to plasma, which is why saline is isotonic. Using the molarity alone (0.15) ignores dissociation.'},
  {ofTag:'Electrochemistry',of:'Galvanic cells house _____ reactions',kind:'application',q:'Which pair of signs is correct for the overall reaction of a working galvanic cell under standard conditions?',choices:['E°cell > 0 and ΔG° < 0','E°cell < 0 and ΔG° > 0','E°cell > 0 and ΔG° > 0','E°cell < 0 and ΔG° < 0'],ans:0,exp:'Galvanic cells run **spontaneous** reactions, and ΔG° = −nFE°cell, so a positive E°cell corresponds to a negative ΔG°. The opposite pair (E° < 0, ΔG° > 0) describes an electrolytic cell; E° and ΔG° never share a sign.'},
  {ofTag:'Electrochemistry',of:'Electrolytic cells house _____ reactions',kind:'except',q:'All of the following are true of an electrolytic cell EXCEPT:',choices:['It requires an external power source','Its overall reaction has a positive E°cell','Reduction occurs at its cathode','Its overall reaction has ΔG > 0'],ans:1,exp:'Electrolytic cells drive **non-spontaneous** reactions, so E°cell is negative and ΔG is positive; an external source must push the electrons. As in every cell, reduction happens at the cathode and oxidation at the anode ("RED CAT, AN OX").'},
  {ofTag:'Electrochemistry',of:'For a concentration cell (identical electrodes and electrolytes in both half-cells), what are the standard values of E°cell and ΔG°?',kind:'prediction',q:'A concentration cell has copper electrodes in 0.010 M Cu²⁺ and 1.0 M Cu²⁺, joined by a wire and a salt bridge. What happens as the cell runs?',choices:['The electrode in 1.0 M Cu²⁺ dissolves, and copper plates out on the dilute side','Copper plates out on both electrodes until both solutions are depleted','The electrode in 0.010 M Cu²⁺ dissolves, and copper plates out on the concentrated side','No current flows, because E°cell = 0'],ans:2,exp:'E°cell is 0, but the actual E = −(0.0592/2)·log([dilute]/[concentrated]) = +0.059 V, so current flows **until the concentrations equalize**: the dilute side is the anode (Cu → Cu²⁺ raises its concentration) and the concentrated side is the cathode (Cu²⁺ → Cu lowers it). A zero standard potential describes equal concentrations, not this cell.'},
  {ofTag:'Electrochemistry',of:'_____ is a measure of the tendency of a chemical species to gain electrons and be reduced',kind:'application',q:'Standard reduction potentials: Ag⁺/Ag = +0.80 V and Zn²⁺/Zn = −0.76 V. In a galvanic cell made from these half-cells, which electrode is the cathode, and what is E°cell?',choices:['Zinc; +1.56 V','Silver; +0.04 V','Zinc; −1.56 V','Silver; +1.56 V'],ans:3,exp:'The species with the **higher reduction potential** (Ag⁺) is reduced at the cathode, and E°cell = E°cathode − E°anode = 0.80 − (−0.76) = **+1.56 V**. Simply adding the two listed values gives +0.04 V; reduction potentials are also intensive, so they are never multiplied by stoichiometric coefficients.'},
  {ofTag:'Reactions & Mechanisms',of:'In a redox reaction, the species that loses electrons is _____',kind:'scenario',q:'In the reaction Zn(s) + Cu²⁺(aq) → Zn²⁺(aq) + Cu(s), which species is the reducing agent?',choices:['Zn(s)','Cu(s)','Cu²⁺(aq)','Zn²⁺(aq)'],ans:0,exp:'Zinc goes from 0 to +2, so it **loses electrons and is oxidized**; the species that is oxidized is the reducing agent because it donates the electrons that reduce Cu²⁺. Cu²⁺ is the oxidizing agent.'},
  {ofTag:'Reactions & Mechanisms',of:'Substitution at a chiral carbon by the _____ mechanism gives a (nearly) racemic mixture of products',kind:'prediction',q:'Optically pure (R)-3-bromo-3-methylhexane is warmed in water, giving 3-methylhexan-3-ol. What is the stereochemical outcome?',choices:['Pure (R) alcohol, by retention','A nearly racemic mixture of the alcohol','An optically inactive meso alcohol','Pure (S) alcohol, by inversion'],ans:1,exp:'A tertiary bromide in a polar protic solvent with a weak nucleophile reacts by **SN1**: the leaving group departs first, forming a planar carbocation that water attacks from either face, so the product is nearly **racemic**. Clean inversion is the SN2 signature, and a product with one stereocenter cannot be meso.'},
  {ofTag:'Reactions & Mechanisms',of:'_____ reactions lead to inversion of stereochemistry at the reacting carbon',kind:'scenario',q:'(S)-2-Bromobutane reacts with NaCN in DMSO. What is the main product?',choices:['A racemic mixture of 2-methylbutanenitrile','But-2-ene, from elimination','(R)-2-methylbutanenitrile','(S)-2-methylbutanenitrile'],ans:2,exp:'A secondary halide with a strong, weakly basic nucleophile in a polar aprotic solvent reacts by **SN2**, whose backside attack inverts the stereocenter. Because –CN takes the top priority that –Br held, the geometric inversion also flips the label from S to **R**. Racemization would indicate SN1, which these conditions do not favor.'},
  {ofTag:'Reactions & Mechanisms',of:'The rate law for an SN2 reaction is _____',kind:'prediction',q:'Bromoethane reacts with hydroxide by an SN2 mechanism. If [OH⁻] is doubled and [CH₃CH₂Br] is halved, how does the initial rate change?',choices:['It doubles','It halves','It quadruples','It does not change'],ans:3,exp:'The SN2 rate law is **rate = k[substrate][nucleophile]**, so the factors multiply: 2 × ½ = 1, leaving the rate unchanged. Treating the reaction as SN1, with a rate that depends only on substrate, would predict halving.'},
  {ofTag:'Reactions & Mechanisms',of:'Strong, bulky bases favor _____ reactions',kind:'scenario',q:'2-Bromo-2-methylbutane is treated with potassium tert-butoxide in tert-butanol. What is the major product?',choices:['2-Methylbut-1-ene','2-Methylbutan-2-ol','tert-Butyl 2-methylbutan-2-yl ether','2-Methylbut-2-ene'],ans:0,exp:'A strong, **bulky base** favors E2 and removes the most accessible β-hydrogen (on a CH₃ group), giving the less substituted **Hofmann** alkene, 2-methylbut-1-ene. A small base such as ethoxide would favor the more substituted Zaitsev product, 2-methylbut-2-ene; the bulk of tert-butoxide also makes substitution negligible.'},
  {ofTag:'Reactions & Mechanisms',of:'_____ solvents are polar but have no O–H or N–H bond, so they cannot donate hydrogen bonds',kind:'application',q:'A chemist wants to maximize the rate of the SN2 reaction between sodium azide and 1-bromobutane. Which solvent is the best choice?',choices:['Methanol','Acetone','Acetic acid','Water'],ans:1,exp:'**Acetone is polar aprotic**: it dissolves the salt but cannot hydrogen-bond to the azide anion, leaving the nucleophile reactive. Water, methanol and acetic acid are polar protic solvents that cage the anion in hydrogen bonds, which slows SN2 (and favors SN1).'},
  {ofTag:'Reactions & Mechanisms',of:'Treating a primary alcohol with pyridinium chlorochromate (PCC) forms a/an _____',kind:'prediction',q:'A chemist treats propan-1-ol with Jones reagent (CrO₃ in aqueous H₂SO₄) instead of PCC. What is the major product?',choices:['Propanal','Propanone','Propanoic acid','Propene'],ans:2,exp:'Jones reagent is a **strong, aqueous oxidant**: the aldehyde formed first is hydrated and oxidized again to the carboxylic acid. PCC in anhydrous CH₂Cl₂ stops at the aldehyde, which is why it is chosen when propanal is wanted; a ketone forms only from a secondary alcohol.'},
  {ofTag:'Carbonyl Chemistry',of:'NaBH₄ is a _____ reducing agent',kind:'application',q:'A molecule contains both a ketone and an ethyl ester. It is treated with NaBH₄ in methanol. What is the most likely result?',choices:['The ester is reduced to an alcohol, and the ketone is unchanged','Both groups are reduced to alcohols','Neither group is reduced','The ketone is reduced to an alcohol, and the ester is unchanged'],ans:3,exp:'**NaBH₄ is a mild hydride donor** that readily reduces aldehydes and ketones but reacts only sluggishly with esters, so it is chemoselective for the ketone. Reducing the ester as well would require the stronger LiAlH₄.'},
  {ofTag:'Carbonyl Chemistry',of:'When two equivalents of alcohol react with a ketone, a/an _____ is formed',kind:'application',q:'Before reducing an ester with LiAlH₄, a chemist protects a ketone in the same molecule by treating it with ethylene glycol (HOCH₂CH₂OH) and catalytic acid. What functional group does the ketone become?',choices:['A cyclic ketal','An enol ether','A cyanohydrin','A hemiketal'],ans:0,exp:'Two alcohol groups add to a ketone carbon, with loss of water, to give a **ketal**; using one diol makes a five-membered cyclic ketal. Ketals are stable to bases and hydride reagents, so the ketone survives the LiAlH₄ step and is regenerated later with aqueous acid. A hemiketal forms after only one alcohol adds and is too unstable to serve as a protecting group.'},
  {ofTag:'Carbonyl Chemistry',of:'Aldehydes are usually _____ reactive toward nucleophilic addition than ketones because of both steric and electronic effects',kind:'prediction',q:'Equimolar acetaldehyde and acetone compete for a small amount of HCN. Which forms more cyanohydrin?',choices:['Acetone','Acetaldehyde','Both form equal amounts','Neither forms a cyanohydrin'],ans:1,exp:'**Aldehydes are more reactive toward nucleophilic addition**: the carbonyl carbon of acetaldehyde carries only one electron-donating alkyl group, so it is more electrophilic and less crowded. The two methyl groups of acetone both donate electron density and hinder attack.'},
  {ofTag:'Carbonyl Chemistry',of:'Removal of a proton from the less substituted (and less hindered) α-carbon gives the _____ enolate',kind:'application',q:'2-Methylcyclohexanone is treated with LDA in THF at −78 °C. Where does deprotonation mainly occur?',choices:['At the carbonyl oxygen','At C2, the more substituted α-carbon','At C6, the less substituted α-carbon','At the methyl group on C2'],ans:2,exp:'LDA is a strong, bulky base, and at low temperature deprotonation is irreversible, so it removes the **more accessible proton on the less substituted α-carbon (C6)**, giving the **kinetic enolate**. Weaker bases at higher temperature allow equilibration to the more substituted, thermodynamic enolate at C2.'},
  {ofTag:'Carbonyl Chemistry',of:'Aldol addition is a powerful way to create a _____ bond',kind:'scenario',q:'Propanal is treated with dilute NaOH and undergoes aldol addition. The new carbon–carbon bond forms between which two atoms?',choices:['The carbonyl carbons of two molecules','The α-carbons of two molecules','The carbonyl oxygen of one molecule and the α-carbon of another','The α-carbon of one molecule and the carbonyl carbon of another'],ans:3,exp:'Base removes an α-hydrogen to make an **enolate**, whose nucleophilic α-carbon attacks the electrophilic carbonyl carbon of a second molecule, forming a **C–C bond** and, after protonation, a β-hydroxy aldehyde. Two carbonyl carbons or two α-carbons cannot bond this way because both partners would have the same polarity.'},
  {ofTag:'Carbonyl Chemistry',of:'_____ is the hydrolysis of the ester bonds of triacylglycerols using a strong base such as NaOH or KOH',kind:'scenario',q:'A triacylglycerol is heated with aqueous NaOH. What are the products?',choices:['Glycerol and three sodium fatty acid carboxylates','Three fatty alcohols and sodium glycerate','A diacylglycerol and one fatty acid','Glycerol and three free fatty acids'],ans:0,exp:'**Saponification** is base-promoted ester hydrolysis: hydroxide cleaves all three ester bonds, and in basic solution the fatty acids are deprotonated to **carboxylate salts (soaps)**, leaving glycerol. Free fatty acids appear only after the mixture is acidified.'},
  {ofTag:'Spectroscopy',of:'In NMR spectroscopy, a peak that is deshielded appears _____',kind:'prediction',q:'Compared with the CH₃ signal of ethane, where does the CH₂ signal of chloroethane (CH₃CH₂Cl) appear in the ¹H NMR spectrum?',choices:['At the same shift, because chlorine bears no hydrogen','Farther downfield, because chlorine deshields it','At 0 ppm, the same as TMS','Farther upfield, because chlorine shields it'],ans:1,exp:'Electronegative chlorine pulls electron density away from the adjacent CH₂, **deshielding** those protons so they resonate at higher δ, **downfield** (about 3.5 ppm versus 0.9 ppm for ethane). Added electron density shields protons and moves signals upfield, toward TMS.'},
  {ofTag:'Spectroscopy',of:'In NMR spectroscopy, the multiplicity of a proton or group of protons is given by the n+1 rule, where n = _____',kind:'application',q:'In the ¹H NMR spectrum of ethyl acetate, CH₃C(=O)OCH₂CH₃, what is the splitting pattern of the –OCH₂– signal?',choices:['Doublet','Triplet','Quartet','Singlet'],ans:2,exp:'The CH₂ protons have three neighbors on the adjacent CH₃, so by the **n + 1 rule** they appear as a 3 + 1 = **quartet**; that CH₃ in turn is a triplet. The acetyl CH₃ has no hydrogens on the neighboring carbonyl carbon, so it is a singlet.'},
  {ofTag:'Spectroscopy',of:'C=O bonds (carbonyl groups) in IR spectroscopy appear at approximately which wavenumber (cm⁻¹)?',kind:'application',q:'A student oxidizes cyclohexanol to cyclohexanone. Which IR change best confirms that the reaction worked?',choices:['A broad 3200–3600 cm⁻¹ peak appears, and the peak near 1715 cm⁻¹ disappears','A peak near 2200 cm⁻¹ appears, and the 3200–3600 cm⁻¹ peak persists','A peak near 1650 cm⁻¹ appears, and the 3200–3600 cm⁻¹ peak persists','A sharp peak near 1715 cm⁻¹ appears, and the broad 3200–3600 cm⁻¹ peak disappears'],ans:3,exp:'The product is a ketone, so a strong, sharp **C=O stretch** (about 1715 cm⁻¹ for cyclohexanone, within the typical 1700–1750 cm⁻¹ carbonyl region) should appear while the broad **O–H stretch** of the alcohol disappears. Peaks near 2200 cm⁻¹ indicate triple bonds, and peaks near 1650 cm⁻¹ indicate C=C.'},
  {ofTag:'Separations & Lab Techniques',of:'In size-exclusion chromatography, large molecules will elute _____',kind:'scenario',q:'A mixture of IgG (150 kDa), hemoglobin (64 kDa) and cytochrome c (12 kDa) is run on a size-exclusion column. In what order do the proteins elute?',choices:['IgG, hemoglobin, cytochrome c','Hemoglobin, IgG, cytochrome c','All three elute together','Cytochrome c, hemoglobin, IgG'],ans:0,exp:'In size-exclusion chromatography small proteins enter the pores of the beads and take a longer path, while **large proteins are excluded and elute first**, so the order is IgG, hemoglobin, then cytochrome c. Reversing the order is the classic error: small molecules are delayed, not sped up.'},
  {ofTag:'Separations & Lab Techniques',of:'Ion exchange chromatography separates proteins by their _____',kind:'application',q:'At pH 7.0, a mixture of protein A (pI 4.5) and protein B (pI 9.0) is loaded onto a positively charged DEAE anion-exchange column. Which protein binds?',choices:['Protein B only','Protein A only','Both proteins','Neither protein'],ans:1,exp:'At a pH above its pI a protein is **net negative**, so protein A (pI 4.5) carries a negative charge at pH 7 and binds the positively charged anion exchanger. Protein B (pI 9.0) is net positive at pH 7 and flows through; it would bind a cation-exchange column instead.'},
  {ofTag:'Separations & Lab Techniques',of:'In an extraction, the aqueous (water) layer is the _____ layer and dissolves ionic and hydrogen-bonding compounds',kind:'scenario',q:'A diethyl ether solution of benzoic acid and naphthalene is shaken with aqueous NaHCO₃. Where is the benzoic acid found afterward?',choices:['In the ether layer, as sodium benzoate','Precipitated as a solid at the interface','In the aqueous layer, as sodium benzoate','In the ether layer, as benzoic acid'],ans:2,exp:'Bicarbonate deprotonates the carboxylic acid (pKa ≈ 4.2) to the **ionic benzoate**, which dissolves in the **polar aqueous layer**; neutral, nonpolar naphthalene stays in the ether. Acidifying the aqueous layer afterward regenerates benzoic acid, which precipitates.'},
  {ofTag:'Separations & Lab Techniques',of:'Simple distillation can be used if the boiling points are under 150 °C and are at least _____ apart',kind:'application',q:'A chemist must separate heptane (bp 98 °C) from toluene (bp 111 °C). Which technique is most appropriate?',choices:['Recrystallization','Simple distillation','Vacuum distillation','Fractional distillation'],ans:3,exp:'The boiling points differ by only 13 °C, less than the roughly **25 °C needed for simple distillation**, so a fractionating column is needed to provide many vaporization–condensation cycles. Vacuum distillation is for compounds boiling above about 150 °C, which these are not.'},
  {ofTag:'Separations & Lab Techniques',of:'Chromatography separates the components of a mixture based on _____',kind:'prediction',q:'A mixture is spotted on a silica gel TLC plate and developed with 9:1 hexane/ethyl acetate. Which compound will have the highest Rf?',choices:['Naphthalene','Benzamide','Benzoic acid','Benzyl alcohol'],ans:0,exp:'Silica is a **polar stationary phase**, so polar compounds that hydrogen-bond to it travel slowly, while nonpolar **naphthalene** has the greatest affinity for the nonpolar mobile phase and moves farthest. Benzoic acid, benzyl alcohol and benzamide all have O–H or N–H groups that hold them near the baseline.'},
  {ofTag:'Bonding & Structure',of:'For a molecule with n chiral centers, the maximum number of possible stereoisomers is _____',kind:'application',q:'The open-chain form of an aldohexose such as glucose has four chiral centers. How many stereoisomeric aldohexoses are possible?',choices:['8','16','32','4'],ans:1,exp:'With no meso forms possible, the maximum is **2ⁿ = 2⁴ = 16** stereoisomers: 8 D-sugars and their 8 L-enantiomers. Answering 8 counts only the D series.'},
  {ofTag:'Bonding & Structure',of:'_____ contain chiral centers but have an internal plane of symmetry, so they are optically inactive',kind:'application',q:'Which of the following compounds is optically inactive even though it has two chiral centers?',choices:['(2S,3S)-tartaric acid','(2R,3S)-2,3-dibromopentane','(2R,3S)-tartaric acid','(2R,3R)-tartaric acid'],ans:2,exp:'(2R,3S)-Tartaric acid has identical halves with opposite configurations, giving an **internal mirror plane**, so it is a **meso compound** and does not rotate light. The (R,R) and (S,S) forms are chiral enantiomers, and 2,3-dibromopentane has different end groups (methyl versus ethyl), so it has no internal mirror plane and is chiral.'},
  {ofTag:'Bonding & Structure',of:'In the chair form of cyclohexane, a bond that points straight up or down, parallel to the ring\'s axis, is in the _____ position',kind:'application',q:'In the most stable chair conformation of tert-butylcyclohexane, where is the tert-butyl group?',choices:['Axial, to avoid 1,3-diaxial interactions','Split evenly between axial and equatorial','Axial, to minimize torsional strain','Equatorial, to avoid 1,3-diaxial interactions'],ans:3,exp:'An **axial** substituent points parallel to the ring axis and clashes with the two axial hydrogens on the same face (**1,3-diaxial strain**); the bulky tert-butyl group avoids this so strongly that it is essentially locked **equatorial**. Smaller groups such as methyl also prefer equatorial, but less overwhelmingly.'},
  {ofTag:'Carbonyl Chemistry',of:'Which ranks carboxylic acid derivatives from most to least reactive toward nucleophilic acyl substitution?',kind:'prediction',q:'Which conversion can be accomplished directly by nucleophilic acyl substitution under mild conditions?',choices:['Acid chloride → ester','Carboxylate → anhydride','Amide → acid chloride','Ester → anhydride'],ans:0,exp:'Nucleophilic acyl substitution runs **downhill in reactivity**, from a more reactive derivative to a less reactive one: acid chloride > anhydride > ester > amide > carboxylate. Treating an acid chloride with an alcohol readily gives an ester; the other conversions go uphill and require activation.'},
  {ofTag:'Intermolecular Forces',of:'For molecules of similar size, which ranking of intermolecular forces runs from strongest to weakest?',kind:'prediction',q:'Ethanol (46 g/mol), dimethyl ether (46 g/mol), propane (44 g/mol) and acetaldehyde (44 g/mol) have similar molar masses. Which has the highest boiling point?',choices:['Dimethyl ether','Ethanol','Propane','Acetaldehyde'],ans:1,exp:'Only **ethanol** has an O–H bond and can **hydrogen bond** with itself, so it boils at 78 °C. Acetaldehyde (20 °C) and dimethyl ether (−24 °C) have dipole–dipole forces but no hydrogen bonding, and nonpolar propane (−42 °C) has only London dispersion forces.'},
  {ofTag:'Fluids',of:'As the cross-sectional area of a Venturi tube decreases, the linear speed must _____',kind:'prediction',q:'Water flows steadily through a horizontal pipe that narrows from a wide section to a constriction. Compared with the wide section, the water in the constriction has:',choices:['lower speed and higher pressure','higher speed and higher pressure','higher speed and lower pressure','the same speed and lower pressure'],ans:2,exp:'By continuity (A₁v₁ = A₂v₂) the smaller area forces the **speed to increase**, and by Bernoulli\'s equation for a horizontal pipe (P + ½ρv² = constant) the faster fluid has **lower pressure**: the Venturi effect. Many students expect pressure to rise where the pipe is squeezed, but it falls.'},
  {ofTag:'Fluids',of:'Volumetric flow rate is the volume of fluid which passes per _____',kind:'application',q:'A garden hose delivers water at a volumetric flow rate of 0.60 L/s through a nozzle with a cross-sectional area of 1.0 cm². What is the speed of the water leaving the nozzle?',choices:['60 m/s','0.06 m/s','0.6 m/s','6 m/s'],ans:3,exp:'Flow rate Q = Av, so v = Q/A = (0.60 × 10⁻³ m³/s)/(1.0 × 10⁻⁴ m²) = **6 m/s**. The key step is unit conversion: 1 L = 10⁻³ m³ and 1 cm² = 10⁻⁴ m².'},
  {ofTag:'Fluids',of:'In a closed fluid system, as the total cross-sectional area increases, the velocity _____',kind:'application',q:'Blood moves fastest in the aorta and slowest in the capillaries, even though each capillary is far narrower than the aorta. What explains this?',choices:['The capillary beds have the largest total cross-sectional area','Blood pressure is highest in the capillaries','Capillary walls let plasma leak out','Each capillary has very high resistance'],ans:0,exp:'Continuity applies to the **total** cross-sectional area at each level of the circulation: billions of capillaries in parallel have a combined area hundreds of times that of the aorta, so **velocity is lowest** there, which allows time for exchange. Using a single capillary\'s area would wrongly predict the fastest flow.'},
  {ofTag:'Fluids',of:'Poiseuille\'s law determines the _____ of laminar flow through a tube',kind:'prediction',q:'According to Poiseuille\'s law, if an arteriole constricts to half its original radius while the pressure difference and blood viscosity stay the same, the flow rate through it becomes:',choices:['1/8 of the original','1/16 of the original','1/2 of the original','1/4 of the original'],ans:1,exp:'Poiseuille\'s law gives Q = πr⁴ΔP/(8ηL), so flow scales with the **fourth power of the radius**: (½)⁴ = **1/16**. This steep dependence is why small changes in arteriolar diameter powerfully regulate blood flow and resistance.'},
  {ofTag:'Fluids',of:'Archimedes\' principle states that the buoyant force equals the _____',kind:'application',q:'A 2.0 kg metal block with a volume of 1.0 × 10⁻³ m³ is held fully submerged in water (ρ = 1000 kg/m³, g = 10 m/s²). What is the buoyant force on it?',choices:['30 N','1 N','10 N','20 N'],ans:2,exp:'The buoyant force equals the **weight of the displaced fluid**: ρ_fluid·V·g = 1000 × 1.0 × 10⁻³ × 10 = **10 N**. The block\'s own mass (and its 20 N weight) does not enter the calculation; that difference is why it sinks, with a net downward force of 10 N.'},
  {ofTag:'Fluids',of:'Specific gravity is the ratio of an object\'s density to _____',kind:'prediction',q:'A solid object with a specific gravity of 0.80 floats in fresh water. What fraction of its volume is submerged?',choices:['100%','20%','64%','80%'],ans:3,exp:'For a floating object the buoyant force equals the weight, so the **fraction submerged equals the specific gravity** (ρ_object/ρ_water) = **80%**. 20% is the fraction above the waterline; an object with specific gravity above 1 sinks completely.'},
  {ofTag:'Fluids',of:'In water, every additional 10 meters of depth adds approximately _____ to the total pressure',kind:'application',q:'A scuba diver descends to a depth of 30 m in the ocean. Approximately what absolute pressure does she experience?',choices:['4 atm','30 atm','31 atm','3 atm'],ans:0,exp:'Each 10 m of water adds about **1 atm** of gauge pressure, so 30 m adds 3 atm; **absolute pressure** also includes the 1 atm of air above the surface, for a total of about 4 atm. Answering 3 atm gives only the gauge pressure.'},
  {ofTag:'Fluids',of:'Hydraulic machines operate based on Pascal\'s principle to generate _____',kind:'application',q:'In a hydraulic lift, the small piston has an area of 0.010 m² and the large piston an area of 0.50 m². A 200 N force on the small piston can support what weight on the large piston?',choices:['200 N','10,000 N','100,000 N','4 N'],ans:1,exp:'Pascal\'s principle transmits the same pressure to both pistons: F₂ = F₁(A₂/A₁) = 200 N × 50 = **10,000 N**. The **mechanical advantage** is not free energy: the small piston must move 50 times farther, so work in equals work out.'},
  {ofTag:'Electricity & Magnetism',of:'The junction rule states that the sum of currents flowing into a junction is _____ the sum flowing out',kind:'application',q:'A 6 Ω resistor and a 3 Ω resistor are connected in parallel across a 12 V battery. What total current leaves the battery?',choices:['2 A','4 A','6 A','1.3 A'],ans:2,exp:'Each branch has the full 12 V across it, so I₆ = 2 A and I₃ = 4 A, and by the **junction rule** the battery supplies their sum, **6 A** (consistent with R_eq = 2 Ω). 1.3 A results from adding the resistors as if they were in series.'},
  {ofTag:'Electricity & Magnetism',of:'The loop rule states that the sum of all electric potential differences around a closed loop is _____',kind:'application',q:'A 12 V battery is connected in series with a 2 Ω resistor and a 4 Ω resistor. What is the voltage drop across the 4 Ω resistor?',choices:['12 V','4 V','6 V','8 V'],ans:3,exp:'Total resistance is 6 Ω, so I = 12/6 = 2 A and V₄ = IR = 2 × 4 = **8 V**. By the **loop rule** the drops (4 V + 8 V) sum to the 12 V supplied; series resistors share voltage in proportion to their resistance.'},
  {ofTag:'Electricity & Magnetism',of:'Ammeters are inserted in series and measure _____',kind:'application',q:'Why should an ideal ammeter have nearly zero resistance?',choices:['It is placed in series, so any resistance would reduce the current being measured','It is placed in series, so low resistance raises the voltage it reads','It is placed in parallel, so low resistance keeps the voltage constant','It is placed in parallel, so low resistance keeps it from drawing current'],ans:0,exp:'An ammeter is inserted **in series**, so all of the circuit current passes through it; any resistance it added would lower that current and distort the reading. A voltmeter, placed in parallel, needs nearly infinite resistance for the analogous reason.'},
  {ofTag:'Electricity & Magnetism',of:'_____ are insulators placed between the plates of a capacitor that increase capacitance',kind:'prediction',q:'A parallel-plate capacitor stays connected to a 9 V battery while a dielectric slab with κ = 3 is inserted between its plates. What happens to the charge stored on the plates?',choices:['It increases ninefold','It triples','It stays the same','It falls to one-third'],ans:1,exp:'A dielectric multiplies capacitance by κ (C = κC₀), and with the battery holding V fixed, **Q = CV triples**. If the capacitor were disconnected first, Q would stay fixed instead and the voltage would fall to one-third.'},
  {ofTag:'Electricity & Magnetism',of:'_____ is the opposition that a substance offers to the flow of electrons',kind:'prediction',q:'A metal wire is drawn out to twice its original length while its volume stays constant. How does its resistance change?',choices:['It is unchanged','It doubles','It quadruples','It halves'],ans:2,exp:'R = ρL/A. Doubling L at constant volume halves A, so **R increases by 2 × 2 = 4**. A common error is to account for the longer length and forget that the cross-section shrinks.'},
  {ofTag:'Electricity & Magnetism',of:'Test charges move spontaneously in the direction that results in _____ in electrical potential energy',kind:'application',q:'An electron is released from rest in a uniform electric field. Which statement describes its motion?',choices:['It moves toward lower potential, and its potential energy decreases','It moves toward higher potential, and its potential energy increases','It moves along an equipotential line, so its energy stays constant','It moves toward higher potential, and its potential energy decreases'],ans:3,exp:'Charges released from rest always move so that their **electrical potential energy decreases**. For a negative charge, U = qV falls as V rises, so the electron moves toward **higher potential**, opposite the field lines; a proton would move toward lower potential.'},
  {ofTag:'Waves, Sound & Light',of:'A convex lens is also known as a _____ lens',kind:'application',q:'An object is placed 30 cm in front of a converging lens with a focal length of 10 cm. Where is the image, and what is it like?',choices:['Real and inverted, 15 cm on the opposite side of the lens','Virtual and upright, 15 cm on the same side as the object','Real and inverted, 7.5 cm on the opposite side of the lens','Virtual and upright, 7.5 cm on the same side as the object'],ans:0,exp:'From 1/f = 1/o + 1/i: 1/i = 1/10 − 1/30 = 2/30, so **i = +15 cm**; a positive image distance for a lens means a **real, inverted** image on the far side, with m = −i/o = −0.5. A converging lens forms a virtual, upright image only when the object is inside the focal length.'},
  {ofTag:'Waves, Sound & Light',of:'A concave lens is also known as a _____ lens',kind:'application',q:'A nearsighted (myopic) patient\'s eye focuses light from distant objects in front of the retina. Which corrective lens is prescribed?',choices:['A converging lens with negative power','A diverging lens with negative power','A converging lens with positive power','A diverging lens with positive power'],ans:1,exp:'A **diverging (concave) lens** spreads incoming rays so they focus farther back, onto the retina; its focal length and power (P = 1/f) are **negative**. Converging lenses with positive power correct farsightedness (hyperopia).'},
  {ofTag:'Waves, Sound & Light',of:'_____ is when light cannot be refracted out of a medium and is reflected back inside',kind:'prediction',q:'Light inside a block of glass (n = 1.5) strikes the glass–air boundary at an angle of incidence of 50°. What happens to the light?',choices:['It refracts into the air, bending toward the normal','It refracts into the air, bending away from the normal','It undergoes total internal reflection','It passes into the air without bending'],ans:2,exp:'The critical angle satisfies sin θc = n₂/n₁ = 1/1.5, so θc ≈ 42°; because 50° exceeds it, no refracted ray can exist and the light undergoes **total internal reflection**, the principle behind fiber optics and endoscopes. Below 42° the light would refract into the air, bending away from the normal.'},
  {ofTag:'Waves, Sound & Light',of:'_____ is a dimensionless number describing how fast light propagates through a material',kind:'prediction',q:'A beam of light passes from air into water (n = 1.33). Which statement is correct?',choices:['Its speed and frequency decrease; its wavelength is unchanged','Its wavelength and frequency decrease; its speed is unchanged','Its speed decreases; its wavelength and frequency increase','Its speed and wavelength decrease; its frequency is unchanged'],ans:3,exp:'A higher **index of refraction** means slower light (v = c/n ≈ 2.25 × 10⁸ m/s in water). Frequency is set by the source and does not change at a boundary, so with v = fλ the wavelength must shrink in proportion to the speed.'},
  {ofTag:'Waves, Sound & Light',of:'By the Doppler effect, the perceived frequency is _____ when the source and detector move toward each other',kind:'scenario',q:'An ambulance with a 700 Hz siren approaches, passes, and then drives away from a pedestrian standing on the sidewalk. What does she hear?',choices:['Above 700 Hz as it approaches, below 700 Hz after it passes','A pitch that rises steadily the whole time','A constant 700 Hz the whole time','Below 700 Hz as it approaches, above 700 Hz after it passes'],ans:0,exp:'By the **Doppler effect**, motion of the source toward the listener compresses the wavefronts and raises the perceived frequency; once the ambulance recedes, the waves are stretched and the perceived frequency drops below 700 Hz. The siren itself always emits 700 Hz.'},
  {ofTag:'Waves, Sound & Light',of:'_____ is the power transmitted per unit area by a sound wave; it increases with amplitude and is perceived as loudness',kind:'prediction',q:'The intensity of a sound increases by a factor of 100. By how much does its sound level increase?',choices:['10 dB','20 dB','100 dB','2 dB'],ans:1,exp:'Sound level is logarithmic in **intensity**, β = 10·log(I/I₀), so a 100-fold increase adds 10 × log(100) = **20 dB**. Each factor of 10 in intensity adds 10 dB.'},
  {ofTag:'Waves, Sound & Light',of:'_____ oscillate in time but the peak amplitude profile does not move in space',kind:'application',q:'A guitar string 0.50 m long, fixed at both ends, vibrates in its fundamental mode. What is the wavelength of the standing wave?',choices:['0.25 m','0.50 m','1.0 m','2.0 m'],ans:2,exp:'A string fixed at both ends has a node at each end, and the fundamental fits **half a wavelength** between them: L = λ/2, so λ = 2L = **1.0 m**. In general, λₙ = 2L/n for strings and for pipes open at both ends.'},
  {ofTag:'Waves, Sound & Light',of:'On a standing wave, _____ are points of maximum oscillation',kind:'application',q:'A pipe 0.85 m long is closed at one end and open at the other. If the speed of sound is 340 m/s, what is its fundamental frequency?',choices:['200 Hz','400 Hz','50 Hz','100 Hz'],ans:3,exp:'The closed end is a displacement node and the open end an **antinode**, so the fundamental is a quarter wavelength: λ = 4L = 3.4 m and f = v/λ = 340/3.4 = **100 Hz**. Treating it as an open pipe (λ = 2L) gives 200 Hz.'},
  {ofTag:'Motion, Forces & Energy',of:'The work done by a conservative force depends only on _____',kind:'application',q:'A 2 kg box is carried from the floor to a shelf 3 m higher, once straight up a ladder and once along a long ramp (g = 10 m/s²). How does the work done by gravity compare for the two paths?',choices:['It is −60 J for both paths','It is −60 J for the ladder and less negative for the ramp','It is more negative for the longer ramp','It is zero for both, because the box starts and ends at rest'],ans:0,exp:'Gravity is a **conservative force**, so its work depends only on the change in height: W = −mgΔh = −(2)(10)(3) = **−60 J** for any path. The net work on the box may be zero, but the work done by gravity alone is not.'},
  {ofTag:'Motion, Forces & Energy',of:'_____ is the transfer of energy by a force acting through a displacement (W = Fd cos θ)',kind:'application',q:'A child pulls a sled 10 m across level snow with a 50 N force directed 60° above the horizontal. How much work does the child do on the sled?',choices:['0 J','250 J','433 J','500 J'],ans:1,exp:'Only the force component along the displacement does work: W = Fd cos θ = 50 × 10 × cos 60° = 50 × 10 × 0.5 = **250 J**. Using sin 60° gives 433 J, and ignoring the angle gives 500 J.'},
  {ofTag:'Motion, Forces & Energy',of:'_____ is the rate at which work is done or energy is transferred',kind:'application',q:'A 60 kg student runs up a flight of stairs 5.0 m high in 10 s (g = 10 m/s²). What average power does she develop against gravity?',choices:['3000 W','30 W','300 W','600 W'],ans:2,exp:'Work against gravity is mgh = 60 × 10 × 5 = 3000 J, and **power = work/time** = 3000 J ÷ 10 s = **300 W**. 3000 W confuses the work (in joules) with the power.'},
  {ofTag:'Motion, Forces & Energy',of:'The efficiency of a machine is defined as _____',kind:'application',q:'A pulley system lifts a 40 kg crate 1.0 m (g = 10 m/s²) while the operator does 500 J of work. What is the efficiency of the system?',choices:['125%','20%','40%','80%'],ans:3,exp:'Useful work output is mgh = 40 × 10 × 1.0 = 400 J, so **efficiency = output/input = 400/500 = 80%**; the remaining 100 J is lost, mostly to friction. Efficiency can never exceed 100%, so the inverted ratio (125%) is impossible.'},
  {ofTag:'Motion, Forces & Energy',of:'No _____ is exchanged in an adiabatic process',kind:'prediction',q:'A gas in a well-insulated cylinder is rapidly compressed by a piston. What happens to its temperature?',choices:['It rises, because work is done on the gas and no heat leaves','It stays the same, because internal energy is conserved','It stays the same, because no heat is exchanged','It falls, because heat escapes during compression'],ans:0,exp:'The process is **adiabatic** (Q = 0), so by the first law all the work done on the gas becomes internal energy: ΔU > 0 and the temperature rises. "No heat exchanged" does not mean constant temperature; constant temperature defines an isothermal process.'},
  {ofTag:'Nuclear & Atomic Physics',of:'_____ is the amount of time required for half of a sample of radioactive nuclei to decay',kind:'application',q:'A hospital receives 80 mg of iodine-131, which has a half-life of 8 days. How much I-131 remains after 24 days?',choices:['40 mg','10 mg','20 mg','26.7 mg'],ans:1,exp:'24 days is three **half-lives**, so the amount halves three times: 80 → 40 → 20 → **10 mg**. Dividing 80 by 3 (26.7 mg) wrongly treats decay as linear.'},
  {ofTag:'Nuclear & Atomic Physics',of:'Alpha decay is the emission of an alpha particle, which is a _____ nucleus',kind:'prediction',q:'Uranium-238 (Z = 92) undergoes alpha decay. What is the daughter nucleus?',choices:['Thorium-238 (Z = 90)','Uranium-234 (Z = 92)','Thorium-234 (Z = 90)','Protactinium-234 (Z = 91)'],ans:2,exp:'An alpha particle is a **helium-4 nucleus** (2 protons, 2 neutrons), so the mass number drops by 4 and the atomic number by 2: ²³⁸₉₂U → ²³⁴₉₀Th + ⁴₂He. Any change in Z changes the element, so uranium-234 cannot be the product.'},
  {ofTag:'Nuclear & Atomic Physics',of:'_____ is a process unstable atoms use to become more stable by drawing an electron into the nucleus',kind:'prediction',q:'Potassium-40 (Z = 19) can decay by electron capture. What is the product nucleus?',choices:['Chlorine-36 (Z = 17)','Potassium-39 (Z = 19)','Calcium-40 (Z = 20)','Argon-40 (Z = 18)'],ans:3,exp:'In **electron capture**, an inner-shell electron combines with a proton to form a neutron, so Z decreases by 1 while the mass number stays the same: ⁴⁰K → ⁴⁰Ar. β⁺ decay causes the same change in Z, whereas calcium-40 is the product of the β⁻ pathway of K-40.'},
  {ofTag:'Nuclear & Atomic Physics',of:'The threshold frequency is the minimum light frequency necessary to _____',kind:'prediction',q:'Light with a frequency below a metal\'s threshold frequency shines on its surface, and no electrons are ejected. What happens if the intensity of this light is doubled?',choices:['Still no electrons are ejected','Electrons are ejected with low kinetic energy','Twice as many electrons are ejected','Electrons are ejected after a short delay'],ans:0,exp:'Each photon carries E = hf, which depends on **frequency, not intensity**; below the threshold frequency no single photon has enough energy to free an electron, however many photons arrive. Above the threshold, greater intensity increases the number of ejected electrons, not their energy.'},
  {ofTag:'Nuclear & Atomic Physics',of:'The work function is the minimum energy necessary to _____',kind:'application',q:'Photons of 5.0 eV strike a metal whose work function is 2.0 eV. What is the maximum kinetic energy of the ejected electrons?',choices:['2.5 eV','3.0 eV','7.0 eV','2.0 eV'],ans:1,exp:'By Einstein\'s photoelectric equation, KE_max = hf − Φ = 5.0 − 2.0 = **3.0 eV**: the **work function** is the minimum energy spent freeing the electron, and the remainder becomes kinetic energy. Adding the energies (7.0 eV) reverses the logic.'},
  {ofTag:'Research Methods & Statistics',of:'A _____ influences both the dependent and independent variable, causing a spurious association',kind:'scenario',q:'A study finds that heavy coffee drinkers have higher rates of lung cancer. Further analysis shows that heavy coffee drinkers are also much more likely to smoke. In this study, smoking is best described as a:',choices:['mediating variable','moderating variable','confounding variable','dependent variable'],ans:2,exp:'Smoking is associated with both the exposure (coffee drinking) and the outcome (lung cancer), creating a spurious association; that makes it a **confounding variable**. A mediator would have to lie on the causal path, with coffee causing smoking, which then causes cancer.'},
  {ofTag:'Research Methods & Statistics',of:'A _____ lies on the causal pathway and explains how one variable affects another',kind:'scenario',q:'Researchers find that regular exercise reduces depressive symptoms, and that it does so by improving sleep quality, which in turn lowers depression. Sleep quality is a:',choices:['moderating variable','control variable','confounding variable','mediating variable'],ans:3,exp:'Sleep quality lies on the **causal pathway** (exercise → better sleep → less depression) and explains how the effect occurs, so it is a **mediator**. A moderator would instead change the strength of the exercise–depression link in different groups.'},
  {ofTag:'Research Methods & Statistics',of:'A _____ influences the strength or direction of a relationship between two other variables',kind:'scenario',q:'A drug lowers blood pressure substantially in patients under 50 but has little effect in patients over 70. In this relationship, age acts as a:',choices:['moderating variable','confounding variable','dependent variable','mediating variable'],ans:0,exp:'Age changes the **strength** of the drug–blood pressure relationship without lying on the causal path, which defines a **moderator** (an interaction effect). A mediator would explain the mechanism through which the drug works.'},
  {ofTag:'Research Methods & Statistics',of:'A _____ is an observational study that follows exposed and unexposed groups over time to compare how often an outcome develops',kind:'scenario',q:'Investigators record the smoking status of 5,000 healthy nurses and follow them for 20 years to compare how often heart disease develops in smokers and nonsmokers. What type of study is this?',choices:['Cross-sectional study','Cohort study','Randomized controlled trial','Case-control study'],ans:1,exp:'Grouping participants by **exposure** and following them forward in time to compare outcome rates defines a prospective **cohort study**. A case-control study starts from the outcome, and a randomized trial would require researchers to assign smoking, which is unethical.'},
  {ofTag:'Research Methods & Statistics',of:'A _____ identifies two existing groups differing in outcome and compares them',kind:'scenario',q:'Researchers identify 200 patients with mesothelioma and 400 similar people without it, then review their work histories for past asbestos exposure. What type of study is this?',choices:['Cross-sectional study','Randomized controlled trial','Case-control study','Cohort study'],ans:2,exp:'Starting from people who already **differ in outcome** and looking back at their exposures is a **case-control study**, well suited to rare diseases such as mesothelioma; it yields odds ratios. A cohort study would start from exposed and unexposed groups and follow them for outcomes.'},
  {ofTag:'Research Methods & Statistics',of:'The _____ is when subject behavior is altered simply by knowing they are being studied',kind:'scenario',q:'Factory workers\' productivity rises whenever researchers visit to observe them, whether the lighting is made brighter or dimmer. Which phenomenon best explains this?',choices:['Social desirability bias','Selection bias','Placebo effect','Hawthorne effect'],ans:3,exp:'The workers changed their behavior simply because **they knew they were being studied**, which is the **Hawthorne effect**, named for the Western Electric plant where it was described. Social desirability bias concerns how people answer questions, and a placebo effect requires an inactive treatment.'},
  {ofTag:'Research Methods & Statistics',of:'If a test says someone has a disease but they do not, this is a type _____ error (false positive)',kind:'scenario',q:'A trial reports that a new drug lowers cholesterol (p = 0.03), and the researchers reject the null hypothesis. Later, larger studies show that the drug has no effect. The original conclusion was a:',choices:['type I error (false positive)','type II error (false negative)','true positive','true negative'],ans:0,exp:'Rejecting a null hypothesis that is actually true is a **type I error**, whose probability is set by α (for example, 0.05). A type II error is the reverse, failing to detect an effect that really exists, which becomes more likely when statistical power is low.'},
  {ofTag:'Research Methods & Statistics',of:'A _____ gives an estimated range of values likely to include an unknown population parameter',kind:'application',q:'A study reports that a drug changes systolic blood pressure by a mean of +2 mmHg, with a 95% confidence interval of −2 to +6 mmHg. What can be concluded at α = 0.05?',choices:['The drug significantly lowers blood pressure','The change is not statistically significant','The drug has been proven to have no effect','The drug significantly raises blood pressure'],ans:1,exp:'Because the **95% confidence interval includes 0** (no difference), the result is not significant at α = 0.05. Failing to reject the null is not proof of no effect: the interval is still compatible with a true change anywhere from −2 to +6 mmHg.'},
  {ofTag:'Research Methods & Statistics',of:'_____ refers to the ability of a study\'s results to be generalized to the population and to other settings',kind:'scenario',q:'A drug trial enrolled only men aged 20 to 30. Physicians question whether its results apply to elderly women. This concern is mainly about the study\'s:',choices:['statistical significance','internal validity','external validity','reliability'],ans:2,exp:'**External validity** is whether results generalize beyond the study sample to other populations and settings. Internal validity asks whether the study itself established a causal effect free of confounding, which a narrow sample does not necessarily threaten.'},
  {ofTag:'Research Methods & Statistics',of:'A right-skewed distribution is also known as _____ skewed',kind:'application',q:'In a town, most households earn between $40,000 and $80,000, but a few earn several million dollars. How do the mean and median household incomes compare?',choices:['The median is greater than the mean','The mean equals the median','The mode is greater than the mean','The mean is greater than the median'],ans:3,exp:'The few very high incomes form a long right tail, a **positively skewed** distribution, which pulls the mean upward while the median stays near the middle of the data; that is why median income is usually reported. In a negatively skewed distribution the mean falls below the median.'},
  {ofTag:'Research Methods & Statistics',of:'The _____ quantifies the spread of data values around the mean in the same units as the data',kind:'application',q:'Scores on an IQ test are normally distributed with a mean of 100 and a standard deviation of 15. About what percentage of people score between 85 and 115?',choices:['68%','95%','99.7%','34%'],ans:0,exp:'85 to 115 spans **one standard deviation** on either side of the mean, which contains about **68%** of a normal distribution (the 68–95–99.7 rule). 34% covers only one side (100 to 115), and 95% corresponds to ±2 SD (70 to 130).'},
  {ofTag:'Research Methods & Statistics',of:'_____ is bias introduced when proper randomization is not achieved',kind:'scenario',q:'To estimate how much the average adult exercises, a researcher surveys people as they leave a fitness center. The estimate is most likely distorted by:',choices:['recall bias','selection bias','detection bias','the Hawthorne effect'],ans:1,exp:'The sample was not randomly drawn from the population; people leaving a gym exercise more than average, so **selection bias** inflates the estimate. Detection bias concerns how intensively an outcome is looked for, not who is sampled.'},
  {ofTag:'Research Methods & Statistics',of:'Which Bradford Hill criterion is the only one considered necessary to establish causation, requiring that the cause precede the effect?',kind:'scenario',q:'A single survey finds that adults with low vitamin D levels report more depressive symptoms, but it cannot show which came first. Which Bradford Hill criterion is this study unable to establish?',choices:['Strength of association','Consistency','Temporality','Biological gradient'],ans:2,exp:'A cross-sectional survey measures exposure and outcome at the same time, so it cannot show that low vitamin D **preceded** depression; **temporality** is the one criterion required for causation. Depression could just as plausibly reduce time outdoors and lower vitamin D.'},
  {ofTag:'Cognition, Learning & Memory',of:'In operant conditioning, "negative" (as in negative reinforcement or negative punishment) means to _____ a stimulus',kind:'scenario',q:'A student with a tension headache takes ibuprofen, and the headache goes away. She is now more likely to take ibuprofen when headaches occur. Which process is illustrated?',choices:['Positive punishment','Negative punishment','Positive reinforcement','Negative reinforcement'],ans:3,exp:'The behavior is strengthened (reinforcement) by **removing** an unpleasant stimulus, the pain, which is **negative reinforcement**. Students often equate "negative" with punishment, but in operant terms negative simply means taking something away.'},
  {ofTag:'Cognition, Learning & Memory',of:'_____ decreases the likelihood of a behavior',kind:'scenario',q:'A teenager loses his phone privileges for a week after coming home past curfew, and afterward he breaks curfew less often. Which process is illustrated?',choices:['Negative punishment','Negative reinforcement','Positive punishment','Extinction'],ans:0,exp:'The behavior decreases (punishment) because something desirable, phone use, is **removed**, which defines **negative punishment**. Negative reinforcement would make a behavior more likely, and extinction means withholding the reinforcer that maintained the behavior rather than taking away an unrelated privilege.'},
  {ofTag:'Cognition, Learning & Memory',of:'When a previously reinforced behavior is no longer reinforced and gradually stops occurring, it has undergone _____',kind:'scenario',q:'A rat learned to press a lever for food pellets. The researcher disconnects the food dispenser, and over many sessions the rat presses the lever less and less until it stops. Which process is illustrated?',choices:['Negative punishment','Extinction','Spontaneous recovery','Habituation'],ans:1,exp:'When reinforcement is no longer delivered, a learned response gradually fades, which is **extinction**. Spontaneous recovery would be the response briefly reappearing after a rest period, and habituation applies to unlearned responses to a repeated stimulus.'},
  {ofTag:'Cognition, Learning & Memory',of:'When reinforcement for a behavior stops, the behavior often temporarily increases in frequency or intensity before it declines. This is known as _____',kind:'scenario',q:'A child usually gets candy by whining at the checkout line. His parents begin ignoring the whining, and for the next few trips it becomes louder and more frequent before it fades. The temporary increase is called:',choices:['stimulus generalization','positive reinforcement','an extinction burst','spontaneous recovery'],ans:2,exp:'When a reinforcer is suddenly withheld, behavior often **escalates briefly before extinguishing**, an **extinction burst**. Parents who give in during the burst reinforce the louder whining and make it harder to extinguish.'},
  {ofTag:'Cognition, Learning & Memory',of:'Stimulus _____ occurs when a person responds to a new stimulus in the same way that they respond to a similar, conditioned stimulus',kind:'scenario',q:'After a child is conditioned to fear a white rat by pairing it with a loud noise, he also shows fear of a white rabbit and a fur coat. Which process does this illustrate?',choices:['Spontaneous recovery','Higher-order conditioning','Stimulus discrimination','Stimulus generalization'],ans:3,exp:'Responding to **new stimuli that resemble the conditioned stimulus** is **stimulus generalization**, as in Watson\'s Little Albert study. Discrimination is the opposite: learning to respond only to the specific conditioned stimulus.'},
  {ofTag:'Cognition, Learning & Memory',of:'_____ is reinforcing behaviors that come closer and closer to the target behavior',kind:'scenario',q:'A trainer wants a dolphin to leap through a hoop. She first rewards the dolphin for swimming near the hoop, then only for swimming through it, then only for leaping through it as it is raised above the water. This technique is:',choices:['shaping','modeling','counterconditioning','chaining'],ans:0,exp:'Reinforcing **successive approximations** of a target behavior is **shaping**. Modeling relies on observing another individual, and chaining links separate learned behaviors in sequence rather than refining a single behavior.'},
  {ofTag:'Cognition, Learning & Memory',of:'_____ is the acquisition of behavior by watching others',kind:'scenario',q:'Children who watch an adult hit and kick an inflatable doll later hit the doll in the same way, even though they were never rewarded for doing so. This is an example of:',choices:['classical conditioning','observational learning','latent learning','operant conditioning'],ans:1,exp:'Bandura\'s Bobo doll experiments showed that behavior can be acquired by **watching a model**, without direct reinforcement, which is **observational learning**. Operant conditioning would require the children\'s own behavior to be reinforced or punished.'},
  {ofTag:'Cognition, Learning & Memory',of:'_____ is a behavioral therapy for phobias and anxiety disorders in which the person learns a relaxation technique and is then gradually exposed to a hierarchy of anxiety-producing stimuli',kind:'scenario',q:'A therapist teaches a patient who fears flying a deep-breathing relaxation technique, then has her stay relaxed while imagining a list of situations ranked from least to most frightening, from booking a ticket to takeoff. Which therapy is this?',choices:["Flooding exposure therapy","Aversive conditioning therapy","Systematic desensitization","Token economy reinforcement"],ans:2,exp:'Pairing a **relaxation response** with a **graded hierarchy** of feared stimuli defines **systematic desensitization**. Flooding exposes the patient to the most feared stimulus at once, without gradual steps.'},
  {ofTag:'Cognition, Learning & Memory',of:'_____ is when new learning makes you forget old memories',kind:'scenario',q:'A student who learned French in high school spends a year studying Spanish. Now, when trying to recall French vocabulary, she keeps producing Spanish words instead. Which phenomenon is this?',choices:['Decay','Source-monitoring error','Proactive interference','Retroactive interference'],ans:3,exp:'**Newer learning (Spanish) disrupts recall of older memories (French)**, so it is **retroactive interference**; "retro" means the interference acts backward in time. Proactive interference would be old French vocabulary intruding while she learned Spanish.'},
  {ofTag:'Cognition, Learning & Memory',of:'_____ is when old memories interfere with learning new memories',kind:'scenario',q:'Months after moving, a woman keeps writing her old address on forms when she means to give her new one. Which phenomenon is this?',choices:['Proactive interference','Retrograde amnesia','Decay','Retroactive interference'],ans:0,exp:'**Old, well-learned information (the old address) interferes with retrieving newer information**, which is **proactive interference**. Retroactive interference runs the other way, with new learning impairing recall of older memories.'},
  {ofTag:'Cognition, Learning & Memory',of:'_____ is the improved recall of specific episodes or information when the external environment at encoding and at retrieval is the same',kind:'scenario',q:'Divers who learned a word list underwater recalled it better underwater than on land, and divers who learned it on land recalled it better on land. Which phenomenon does this demonstrate?',choices:['State-dependent memory','Context-dependent memory','The spacing effect','The serial position effect'],ans:1,exp:'Recall improved when the **external environment** at retrieval matched the one at encoding, which is **context-dependent memory** (Godden and Baddeley), an example of encoding specificity. State-dependent memory refers to a matching internal state, such as mood or intoxication.'},
  {ofTag:'Cognition, Learning & Memory',of:'The _____ is the phenomenon whereby learning is greater when study sessions are spread out over time rather than massed together',kind:'scenario',q:'Two students each study the same material for six hours. One studies one hour a day for six days; the other studies for six hours the night before the exam. The first student retains much more a month later. This illustrates the:',choices:['serial position effect','self-reference effect','spacing effect','testing effect'],ans:2,exp:'Distributing study across several sessions produces better long-term retention than **massed practice** (cramming), which is the **spacing effect**. Total study time was identical, so the difference comes from the spacing alone.'},
  {ofTag:'Biological Bases of Behavior',of:'The _____ is responsible for consolidating short-term memories into long-term memories',kind:'application',q:'After surgery that removed parts of both medial temporal lobes, a patient could no longer form new memories of facts or events, yet he still improved at a mirror-drawing task over several days. Damage to which structure best explains his memory loss?',choices:['Basal ganglia','Amygdala','Cerebellum','Hippocampus'],ans:3,exp:'The **hippocampus** consolidates new explicit (declarative) memories into long-term storage, so bilateral damage produces anterograde amnesia, as in patient H.M. His preserved motor learning is procedural (implicit) memory, which depends on the cerebellum and basal ganglia rather than the hippocampus.'},
  {ofTag:'Personality, Development & Disorders',of:'The memory loss of Korsakoff syndrome is caused by a deficiency of _____',kind:'application',q:'A 55-year-old man with long-standing alcohol use disorder has severe anterograde amnesia and fills memory gaps with fabricated stories (confabulation). Which supplement, given earlier, might have prevented this condition?',choices:['Vitamin B1 (thiamine)','Vitamin B3 (niacin)','Vitamin B9 (folate)','Vitamin B12 (cobalamin)'],ans:0,exp:'This is **Korsakoff syndrome**, caused by **thiamine deficiency**, which is common in chronic alcohol use and often follows untreated Wernicke encephalopathy; thiamine is given before glucose in malnourished patients. B12 deficiency causes a different picture, with megaloblastic anemia and spinal cord degeneration.'},
  {ofTag:'Cognition, Learning & Memory',of:'The _____ is a mental shortcut that relies on the examples that come to mind most easily when evaluating a situation',kind:'scenario',q:'After seeing extensive news coverage of a plane crash, a man decides to drive across the country instead of flying because he believes flying is more dangerous, even though driving carries a higher risk per mile. Which mental shortcut best explains his judgment?',choices:['Representativeness heuristic','Availability heuristic','Anchoring heuristic','Framing effect'],ans:1,exp:'He judged likelihood by **how easily vivid examples came to mind**, which is the **availability heuristic**. The representativeness heuristic instead judges by resemblance to a prototype, as when a quiet person is assumed to be a librarian.'},
  {ofTag:'Cognition, Learning & Memory',of:'_____ is the tendency to use objects only in the way they are normally used',kind:'scenario',q:'Participants given a candle, a box of thumbtacks and matches are asked to mount the candle on a wall. Most fail to realize they can empty the box and tack it to the wall as a shelf. Which obstacle to problem solving is this?',choices:['Confirmation bias','Belief perseverance','Functional fixedness','The availability heuristic'],ans:2,exp:'Seeing the box only as a container for tacks, its usual function, is **functional fixedness** (Duncker\'s candle problem). A mental set is the related tendency to reuse a problem-solving approach that worked before, rather than a fixed view of an object\'s use.'},
  {ofTag:'Cognition, Learning & Memory',of:'_____ occurs when people make a decision based on the way information is presented rather than on the facts themselves',kind:'scenario',q:'Patients are more likely to choose a surgery described as having a "90% survival rate" than the same surgery described as having a "10% mortality rate." Which bias is this?',choices:['Hindsight bias','Anchoring bias','Confirmation bias','Framing bias'],ans:3,exp:'The facts are identical; only the **presentation** differs, so the shift in choices reflects **framing**. People tend to avoid risk when options are framed as gains (survival) and to seek risk when the same options are framed as losses (death).'},
  {ofTag:'Sensation & Perception',of:'Weber\'s law states that the just-noticeable difference for a stimulus is proportional to the _____ of the stimulus',kind:'application',q:'A person can just barely tell that a 50 g weight is heavier when 1 g is added. According to Weber\'s law, about how much must be added to a 200 g weight for the difference to be noticed?',choices:['4 g','8 g','1 g','2 g'],ans:0,exp:'Weber\'s law says the **just-noticeable difference is a constant fraction of the stimulus magnitude**: 1/50 = 2%, and 2% of 200 g is **4 g**. Answering 1 g assumes the JND is a fixed amount rather than a fixed proportion.'},
  {ofTag:'Sensation & Perception',of:'In signal detection theory, a _____ response strategy is one in which the observer says "yes" whenever there may be a signal',kind:'scenario',q:'Worried about missing early tumors, a radiologist labels every ambiguous shadow on a mammogram as possibly cancerous. As a result, she has more hits but also more false alarms. Which response strategy is she using?',choices:['A conservative response strategy','A liberal response strategy','A lower absolute threshold','Greater sensory adaptation'],ans:1,exp:'In signal detection theory, a **liberal strategy** says "yes" whenever a signal might be present, which raises hits and false alarms together. Her sensory sensitivity has not changed; her decision criterion has, shaped by the high cost of a miss.'},
  {ofTag:'Sensation & Perception',of:'_____ proposes that we hear different pitches because different sound frequencies maximally vibrate different places along the cochlea\'s basilar membrane',kind:'prediction',q:'A patient has damage restricted to the base of the cochlea, near the oval window. According to place theory, which hearing deficit is most likely?',choices:['Loss of the sense of balance','Loss of low-frequency hearing','Loss of high-frequency hearing','Loss of hearing at all frequencies'],ans:2,exp:'**Place theory** holds that each frequency maximally vibrates a particular spot on the basilar membrane: the stiff, narrow **base responds to high frequencies**, and the wide, floppy apex to low ones. This is why noise exposure and aging typically cause high-frequency loss first.'},
  {ofTag:'Sensation & Perception',of:'The _____ states that the visual system codes color in three opposing pairs: blue/yellow, red/green, and black/white',kind:'scenario',q:'After staring at a bright red square for a minute, a person looks at a white wall and sees a green square. Which theory best explains this afterimage?',choices:['Place theory','Signal detection theory','Trichromatic theory','Opponent-process theory'],ans:3,exp:'The **opponent-process theory** codes color in opposing pairs (red/green, blue/yellow, black/white); fatiguing the red side of the red–green channel lets the green side dominate when neutral white is viewed. The trichromatic theory explains color detection by three cone types but cannot account for complementary afterimages.'},
  {ofTag:'Cognition, Learning & Memory',of:'_____ is a neuropsychological condition in which damage to the right parietal lobe causes a person to ignore the left side of their world, and even the left side of their own body',kind:'application',q:'After a stroke, a patient eats only the food on the right side of her plate, shaves only the right side of her face, and draws clocks with all the numbers crowded onto the right half. Where is the lesion most likely located?',choices:['Right parietal lobe','Left temporal lobe','Right cerebellum','Left occipital lobe'],ans:0,exp:'This is **hemispatial neglect**, typically caused by damage to the **right parietal lobe**, which attends to both sides of space; the left parietal lobe attends mainly to the right side, so left-sided lesions rarely cause lasting neglect. An occipital lesion would cause a visual field cut that patients notice and compensate for.'},
  {ofTag:'Cognition, Learning & Memory',of:'_____ states that all sensory information enters a sensory buffer, and a filter then selects one input for further processing based on its physical characteristics, before its meaning is analyzed',kind:'scenario',q:'In a dichotic listening task, participants repeat the message played to their right ear. Many still notice when their own name is spoken in the unattended left ear. This finding is hardest to explain with which theory?',choices:['The resource model of attention','Broadbent\'s early selection theory','Treisman\'s attenuation theory','Deutsch and Deutsch late selection theory'],ans:1,exp:'**Broadbent\'s filter** blocks unattended input on the basis of physical features **before its meaning is analyzed**, so it predicts that nothing meaningful, including one\'s own name, should get through. Treisman\'s attenuation theory (unattended input is weakened, not blocked) and late selection theory (all input is analyzed for meaning) both accommodate this cocktail party effect.'},
  {ofTag:'Personality, Development & Disorders',of:'Piaget\'s _____ is characterized by pretend play, symbolic thinking, egocentrism, and centration',kind:'scenario',q:'A 4-year-old watches juice poured from a short, wide glass into a tall, thin glass and insists that the tall glass now holds more juice. In which of Piaget\'s stages is this child?',choices:['Formal operational','Sensorimotor','Preoperational','Concrete operational'],ans:2,exp:'Failure of **conservation**, driven by centration on a single dimension (height), is a hallmark of the **preoperational stage** (about ages 2–7). Children in the concrete operational stage (about 7–11) understand that pouring does not change the amount.'},
  {ofTag:'Personality, Development & Disorders',of:'Piaget\'s _____ is characterized by abstract thought, systematic problem solving, and moral reasoning',kind:'scenario',q:'Asked what determines how fast a pendulum swings, a 15-year-old varies the string length, weight and release height one at a time while holding the others constant. Which of Piaget\'s stages does this reflect?',choices:['Sensorimotor','Preoperational','Concrete operational','Formal operational'],ans:3,exp:'Generating hypotheses and testing variables systematically is a hallmark of the **formal operational stage** (about age 12 and up), shown in Inhelder and Piaget\'s pendulum task. Concrete operational children reason logically about concrete objects but tend to change several variables at once.'},
  {ofTag:'Personality, Development & Disorders',of:'Each of Erikson\'s 8 stages of psychosocial development is defined by a _____ that must be resolved',kind:'scenario',q:'A 72-year-old man reflects on his life with deep regret, believing he wasted his opportunities and that it is too late to change anything. According to Erikson, which conflict is he struggling with?',choices:['Integrity versus despair','Identity versus role confusion','Generativity versus stagnation','Intimacy versus isolation'],ans:0,exp:'Erikson\'s final stage, in late adulthood, is **integrity versus despair**: reviewing one\'s life with acceptance yields wisdom, whereas regret yields despair. Generativity versus stagnation belongs to middle adulthood and concerns contributing to the next generation.'},
  {ofTag:'Personality, Development & Disorders',of:'A child who displays Kohlberg\'s _____ morality will care for others and obey rules simply because they exist',kind:'scenario',q:'Asked why stealing is wrong, a teenager answers: "Because it is against the law, and if everyone ignored laws, society would fall apart." Which level of Kohlberg\'s moral reasoning does this reflect?',choices:['Preconventional: seeking personal gain','Conventional: maintaining social order','Postconventional: universal ethical principles','Preconventional: avoiding punishment'],ans:1,exp:'Reasoning based on **upholding laws to maintain social order** is Kohlberg\'s **conventional** level (the law-and-order stage). Preconventional reasoning centers on punishment or personal reward, and postconventional reasoning appeals to principles that can justify breaking an unjust law.'},
  {ofTag:'Social Psychology & Society',of:'_____ leads a child to become distressed when a caregiver leaves and to be ambivalent when the caregiver returns',kind:'scenario',q:'In the Strange Situation, a toddler becomes extremely distressed when her mother leaves. When the mother returns, the child clings to her but also pushes her away angrily and is hard to comfort. Which attachment style is this?',choices:['Secure attachment','Avoidant attachment','Ambivalent attachment','Disorganized attachment'],ans:2,exp:'Intense distress at separation combined with **both clinging and angry resistance at reunion** defines **ambivalent (anxious-resistant) attachment**, often linked to inconsistent caregiving. Securely attached children are distressed but easily comforted, and disorganized children show no consistent pattern.'},
  {ofTag:'Social Psychology & Society',of:'According to the _____, individuals in a group are less likely to help a person in need than individuals who are alone',kind:'scenario',q:'A man collapses on a crowded subway platform, and dozens of commuters walk past without helping. When the same event is staged on a nearly empty platform, the lone witness helps within seconds. Which phenomenon explains the difference?',choices:['Groupthink','Social loafing','Deindividuation','The bystander effect'],ans:3,exp:'The more people present, the less likely any one person is to help: the **bystander effect**, driven largely by **diffusion of responsibility** and uncertainty about whether help is needed. Social loafing concerns reduced effort on shared group tasks, not helping in emergencies.'},
  {ofTag:'Social Psychology & Society',of:'_____ describes the tendency of people to perform at a different level when others are around',kind:'scenario',q:'An experienced pianist plays a well-rehearsed piece better in front of an audience than when practicing alone, but a beginner makes more mistakes when others are watching. Which phenomenon explains both observations?',choices:['Social facilitation','Social loafing','Deindividuation','Group polarization'],ans:0,exp:'The presence of others raises arousal, which **improves performance on simple or well-learned tasks** and impairs it on difficult or new ones; this is **social facilitation** (Zajonc). Social loafing is reduced effort when individual contributions are pooled and hard to identify.'},
  {ofTag:'Social Psychology & Society',of:'_____ is the tendency for groups to make decisions based on ideas and solutions that arise within the group without considering outside ideas',kind:'scenario',q:'A presidential cabinet, eager to maintain harmony and loyalty to its leader, approves a risky plan. Members with private doubts stay silent, and no outside experts are consulted. Which phenomenon is this?',choices:['Group polarization','Groupthink','Social facilitation','The bystander effect'],ans:1,exp:'Valuing **consensus over critical evaluation**, with self-censorship and insulation from outside views, defines **groupthink**; Janis\'s classic example is the Bay of Pigs invasion. Group polarization describes discussion pushing members toward a more extreme version of their initial views.'},
  {ofTag:'Social Psychology & Society',of:'_____ is the bias toward making dispositional rather than situational attributions about the actions of others',kind:'scenario',q:'A driver is cut off in traffic and immediately concludes that the other driver is a reckless, selfish person, without considering that he might be rushing to a hospital. Which bias is this?',choices:['Halo effect','Self-serving bias','Fundamental attribution error','Just-world phenomenon'],ans:2,exp:'Overestimating **dispositional** causes and underestimating **situational** ones when explaining someone else\'s behavior is the **fundamental attribution error**. Self-serving bias concerns explanations of one\'s own successes and failures.'},
  {ofTag:'Social Psychology & Society',of:'_____ is the tendency to attribute your own actions to external causes and other people\'s actions to dispositional causes',kind:'scenario',q:'When Maya fails an exam, she blames the confusing questions; when her roommate fails the same exam, Maya concludes the roommate is lazy. Which bias does this pattern illustrate?',choices:['Just-world phenomenon','Halo effect','Fundamental attribution error','Actor-observer bias'],ans:3,exp:'Attributing **one\'s own behavior to the situation but others\' identical behavior to their dispositions** is the **actor-observer bias**. The fundamental attribution error describes only the observer half of this pattern.'},
  {ofTag:'Emotion, Motivation & Stress',of:'_____ is the mental discomfort experienced by a person who holds two or more contradictory beliefs, ideas, or values, or whose behavior conflicts with their beliefs',kind:'scenario',q:'Participants paid $1 to tell others that a boring task was fun later rated the task as more enjoyable than participants paid $20 to tell the same lie. Which concept best explains this result?',choices:['Cognitive dissonance','Fundamental attribution error','Social facilitation','Group polarization'],ans:0,exp:'Lying for only $1 created tension between behavior and belief without enough external justification, so participants reduced the **dissonance** by changing their attitude toward the task (Festinger and Carlsmith). The $20 group could justify the lie by the payment and felt little dissonance.'},
  {ofTag:'Social Psychology & Society',of:'_____ occurs when a person conforms with a group in order to be liked and accepted by them',kind:'scenario',q:'In Asch\'s line-judgment study, many participants agreed with a group\'s obviously wrong answer, yet later said privately that they knew the group was wrong. What type of social influence does this reflect?',choices:['Informational social influence','Normative social influence','Obedience to authority','Internalization'],ans:1,exp:'Conforming publicly to **gain acceptance and avoid rejection** while privately disagreeing reflects **normative social influence**, which produces compliance. Informational influence occurs when people believe the group actually knows better, as in ambiguous situations, and it leads to internalization.'},
  {ofTag:'Emotion, Motivation & Stress',of:'_____ is a change in behavior based on a command from someone seen as an authority figure',kind:'scenario',q:'In Milgram\'s study, most participants kept delivering what they believed were dangerous electric shocks because an experimenter in a lab coat told them, "The experiment requires that you continue." This is an example of:',choices:['conformity','compliance','obedience','group polarization'],ans:2,exp:'Changing behavior in response to a **command from a perceived authority figure** is **obedience**. Compliance involves a request from someone without special authority, and conformity involves matching group behavior without any direct request.'},
  {ofTag:'Social Psychology & Society',of:'A _____ is when stereotypes lead to expectations, and those expectations create conditions that confirm the stereotype',kind:'scenario',q:'Teachers were told that certain randomly chosen students were about to "bloom" intellectually. By year\'s end, those students showed larger IQ gains, apparently because teachers gave them more attention and encouragement. Which phenomenon does this illustrate?',choices:['Halo effect','Just-world phenomenon','Stereotype threat','Self-fulfilling prophecy'],ans:3,exp:'**Expectations changed the teachers\' behavior, which in turn produced the expected outcome**: a **self-fulfilling prophecy**, demonstrated in the Rosenthal–Jacobson (Pygmalion) study. Stereotype threat is a performer\'s own anxiety about confirming a negative stereotype about their group.'},
  {ofTag:'Emotion, Motivation & Stress',of:'_____ explains that stressors do not produce specific responses; they all generate the same general physical stress response',kind:'scenario',q:'After months of caring for a seriously ill parent while working full time, a woman catches frequent colds and feels physically depleted. According to the general adaptation syndrome, which stage is she most likely in?',choices:['Exhaustion','Recovery','Alarm','Resistance'],ans:0,exp:'Selye\'s **general adaptation syndrome** runs from alarm to resistance to **exhaustion**; prolonged stress eventually depletes the body\'s resources and suppresses immune function, making illness more likely. In the resistance stage, the body sustains its stress response and is still coping.'},
  {ofTag:'Emotion, Motivation & Stress',of:'The _____ states that behavioral and physiological responses lead to emotions',kind:'scenario',q:'A hiker sees a bear, starts running, notices her pounding heart, and only then feels afraid. Which theory of emotion does this sequence match?',choices:['Cannon-Bard theory','James-Lange theory','Schachter-Singer theory','Lazarus theory'],ans:1,exp:'The **James-Lange theory** holds that the **physiological response comes first** and the emotion is the perception of that response ("I am afraid because I am running"). Cannon-Bard holds that the arousal and the emotion occur simultaneously and independently.'},
  {ofTag:'Emotion, Motivation & Stress',of:'The _____ states that physiological arousal plus a cognitive label based on context leads to emotion',kind:'scenario',q:'Participants injected with epinephrine, and not told about its effects, felt happy when waiting with a cheerful confederate but angry when waiting with an irritable one. Which theory does this support?',choices:['James-Lange theory','Cannon-Bard theory','Schachter-Singer theory','Opponent-process theory'],ans:2,exp:'The same physiological arousal led to different emotions depending on the **cognitive label** drawn from the social context, which supports the **Schachter-Singer two-factor theory**. James-Lange would predict that identical arousal produces the same emotion.'},
  {ofTag:'Emotion, Motivation & Stress',of:'The _____ describes the relationship between arousal and performance: increased arousal improves performance, but only up to an optimal point, beyond which performance declines',kind:'prediction',q:'According to the Yerkes-Dodson law, how does the optimal level of arousal for a difficult, unfamiliar task compare with that for a simple, well-practiced task?',choices:['It is higher','It is the same','No optimal level exists','It is lower'],ans:3,exp:'Performance follows an inverted U, and for **complex tasks the peak occurs at lower arousal**, because high arousal narrows attention and disrupts difficult thinking; simple tasks tolerate, or even benefit from, higher arousal. This is why mild exam nerves can help while panic hurts.'},
  {ofTag:'Emotion, Motivation & Stress',of:'_____ states that people have certain needs arranged in a hierarchy, with some needs being more primitive or basic than others',kind:'application',q:'A refugee arrives at a shelter hungry and exhausted after days of travel. According to Maslow\'s hierarchy of needs, which need will most likely dominate his behavior first?',choices:['Physiological needs','Esteem','Belongingness and love','Safety'],ans:0,exp:'Maslow placed **physiological needs** (food, water, sleep) at the base of the hierarchy; they must be largely met before higher needs such as safety, belonging and esteem strongly motivate behavior. Safety is the next level up.'},
  {ofTag:'Personality, Development & Disorders',of:'_____ disorder requires at least one major depressive episode and at least one hypomanic episode, but no manic episode',kind:'application',q:'A 28-year-old has had two major depressive episodes and one 5-day period of elevated mood, decreased need for sleep and talkativeness that did not impair her work or require hospitalization. She has never had a manic episode. Which diagnosis fits best?',choices:['Bipolar I disorder','Bipolar II disorder','Cyclothymic disorder','Major depressive disorder'],ans:1,exp:'At least one **major depressive episode plus at least one hypomanic episode** (4 or more days, without marked impairment), and **no manic episode**, defines **bipolar II disorder**. A single full manic episode would make the diagnosis bipolar I, and cyclothymia never reaches full depressive-episode criteria.'},
  {ofTag:'Personality, Development & Disorders',of:'Persistent depressive disorder (dysthymia) is a depressed mood present most of the day, on more days than not, for at least _____ years in adults',kind:'application',q:'For the past 3 years, a 40-year-old has felt depressed on most days, with low energy and poor self-esteem, but has never met full criteria for a major depressive episode. Which diagnosis is most appropriate?',choices:['Adjustment disorder','Major depressive disorder','Persistent depressive disorder','Cyclothymic disorder'],ans:2,exp:'A depressed mood on more days than not for **at least 2 years** in adults defines **persistent depressive disorder (dysthymia)**. Cyclothymia requires both hypomanic and depressive symptoms, and adjustment disorder is a time-limited response to an identifiable stressor.'},
  {ofTag:'Personality, Development & Disorders',of:'If a person has persistent difficulty recovering (for more than a month) after experiencing or witnessing a terrifying event, they may develop _____',kind:'application',q:'Two weeks after a violent assault, a woman has nightmares, flashbacks and hypervigilance, and she avoids the street where it happened. Based on the duration of her symptoms, which diagnosis is most appropriate?',choices:['Generalized anxiety disorder','Adjustment disorder','Posttraumatic stress disorder','Acute stress disorder'],ans:3,exp:'Trauma-related symptoms lasting **3 days to 1 month** after the event meet criteria for **acute stress disorder**, because **PTSD requires symptoms lasting more than 1 month**. If her symptoms persist beyond a month, the diagnosis would change to PTSD.'},
  {ofTag:'Personality, Development & Disorders',of:'_____ symptoms of schizophrenia add something to behavior, cognition, or affect, such as delusions or hallucinations',kind:'except',q:'Each of the following is a positive symptom of schizophrenia EXCEPT:',choices:['Avolition','Disorganized speech','Auditory hallucinations','Persecutory delusions'],ans:0,exp:'**Avolition** (lack of motivation) is a **negative symptom**, an absence of normal function, along with flat affect, alogia and anhedonia. Positive symptoms add abnormal experiences or behaviors, such as hallucinations, delusions and disorganized speech.'},
  {ofTag:'Personality, Development & Disorders',of:'_____ (functional neurological symptom disorder) involves neurological symptoms such as paralysis or blindness that are incompatible with any recognized medical condition',kind:'application',q:'After witnessing her son\'s car accident, a woman suddenly cannot move her legs. Her reflexes, nerve studies and spinal imaging are all normal, and she is not intentionally producing the symptom. Which diagnosis is most likely?',choices:['Factitious disorder','Conversion disorder','Somatic symptom disorder','Illness anxiety disorder'],ans:1,exp:'A neurological symptom (paralysis) that is **incompatible with recognized neurological disease**, often following a stressor, and not intentionally produced fits **conversion disorder (functional neurological symptom disorder)**. Factitious disorder involves deliberately faking or inducing symptoms.'},
  {ofTag:'Personality, Development & Disorders',of:'_____ is a mental disorder in which a person deliberately falsifies or induces symptoms of physical or mental illness even without obvious external rewards',kind:'application',q:'A nurse is repeatedly hospitalized for low blood sugar. Investigation reveals that she secretly injects herself with insulin, although she gains no money, time off or other external reward. Which diagnosis fits best?',choices:['Illness anxiety disorder','Malingering','Factitious disorder','Conversion disorder'],ans:2,exp:'**Deliberately inducing symptoms without external incentives**, apparently to occupy the sick role, defines **factitious disorder**. Malingering also involves intentionally producing symptoms but is motivated by external rewards such as money or avoiding work.'},
  {ofTag:'Personality, Development & Disorders',of:'Signs and biological findings of _____ include bradykinesia, resting (pill-rolling) tremor, masklike facies, cogwheel rigidity, shuffling gait, and low dopamine',kind:'application',q:'A 68-year-old man has a resting pill-rolling tremor, slowed movements and a shuffling gait. Which treatment directly addresses the underlying neurotransmitter deficit?',choices:['A dopamine receptor antagonist','A cholinesterase inhibitor','A selective serotonin reuptake inhibitor','Levodopa (L-DOPA)'],ans:3,exp:'These are signs of **Parkinson\'s disease**, caused by loss of dopaminergic neurons in the substantia nigra; **L-DOPA** crosses the blood–brain barrier and is converted to dopamine. Dopamine antagonists (antipsychotics) can actually cause parkinsonism, and cholinesterase inhibitors are used in Alzheimer\'s disease.'},
  {ofTag:'Personality, Development & Disorders',of:'Difficulty producing speech, with comprehension intact, is called _____',kind:'application',q:'After a stroke, a patient understands spoken questions and follows commands but speaks slowly and effortfully in short phrases, such as "Walk... dog... park," and is visibly frustrated. Which condition is this?',choices:['Broca\'s aphasia','Conduction aphasia','Global aphasia','Wernicke\'s aphasia'],ans:0,exp:'**Nonfluent, effortful speech with intact comprehension**, and awareness of the deficit, is **Broca\'s (expressive) aphasia**, from damage to the left inferior frontal lobe. Wernicke\'s aphasia is the reverse: fluent but meaningless speech, poor comprehension and little awareness.'},
  {ofTag:'Biological Bases of Behavior',of:'The part of the nervous system responsible for the fight-or-flight response is the _____',kind:'except',q:'Activation of the sympathetic nervous system produces each of the following EXCEPT:',choices:['Bronchodilation','Increased digestive motility','Pupil dilation','Increased heart rate'],ans:1,exp:'The **sympathetic** (fight-or-flight) system diverts resources to immediate action and **inhibits digestion**; increased gut motility and secretion are parasympathetic (rest-and-digest) effects. Pupil dilation, a faster heart rate and bronchodilation all prepare the body to respond to a threat.'},
  {ofTag:'Biological Bases of Behavior',of:'Absence or surgical cutting of the _____ can result in split-brain syndrome, in which each hemisphere of the brain functions largely independently',kind:'prediction',q:'A split-brain patient stares at a central point while a picture of a key is flashed only to his left visual field. What is he most likely able to do?',choices:['Name the key aloud, but not pick it out by touch','Name the key aloud and pick it out with his right hand','Pick out the key with his left hand, but not name it','Neither name the key nor pick it out'],ans:2,exp:'The left visual field projects to the **right hemisphere**, which controls the **left hand** but usually lacks speech production; with the **corpus callosum** cut, the information cannot reach the left hemisphere\'s language areas. He can therefore pick out the key by touch with his left hand while saying he saw nothing.'},
  {ofTag:'Biological Bases of Behavior',of:'Slow-wave sleep (SWS) corresponds to sleep stage _____',kind:'scenario',q:'A child sleepwalks, usually during the first third of the night, while the EEG shows high-amplitude, low-frequency delta waves. In which sleep stage do these episodes occur?',choices:['REM','N1','N2','N3'],ans:3,exp:'**Delta waves** mark **N3 (slow-wave) sleep**, which is concentrated early in the night; sleepwalking and night terrors arise from this stage. REM sleep, when vivid dreaming occurs, has a beta-like EEG and muscle atonia that normally prevents acting out movements.'},
  {ofTag:'Biological Bases of Behavior',of:'_____ triggers sleepiness',kind:'application',q:'Using a phone or tablet late at night can delay sleep onset because its blue light suppresses the pineal gland\'s secretion of which hormone?',choices:['Melatonin','Serotonin','Orexin','Cortisol'],ans:0,exp:'Light detected by retinal ganglion cells signals the suprachiasmatic nucleus to **suppress melatonin**, the pineal hormone that promotes sleepiness as darkness falls. Cortisol rises in the early morning and promotes wakefulness, and orexin is a wake-promoting peptide made in the hypothalamus, not the pineal gland.'},
  {ofTag:'Social Psychology & Society',of:'_____ are the unexpected, unintended, or unrecognized consequences of manifest actions',kind:'scenario',q:'Colleges exist to educate students, but they also bring together young adults who often meet future spouses and form lifelong professional networks. In functionalist terms, these social outcomes are:',choices:['manifest functions','latent functions','social dysfunctions','social facts'],ans:1,exp:'Unintended or unrecognized consequences of an institution are **latent functions**; education itself is the **manifest**, intended function. A dysfunction would be a consequence that disrupts the social system.'},
  {ofTag:'Social Psychology & Society',of:'_____, rooted in the work of Karl Marx, claims that society is in a state of perpetual conflict because of competition for limited resources',kind:'scenario',q:'A sociologist studies how wealthy families use private tutoring, legacy admissions and donations to secure elite college spots, preserving their advantage over poorer families. Which theoretical perspective is she using?',choices:['Functionalism','Symbolic interactionism','Conflict theory','Rational choice theory'],ans:2,exp:'Focusing on how groups **compete for scarce resources** and how the powerful maintain their advantage is **conflict theory**, rooted in Marx. Functionalism would instead ask how college admissions contribute to social stability.'},
  {ofTag:'Social Psychology & Society',of:'_____ is a position in a social group that one earns based on merit or one\'s choices',kind:'except',q:'Each of the following is an achieved status EXCEPT:',choices:['Olympic athlete','Physician','College graduate','Ethnicity'],ans:3,exp:'**Ethnicity** is an **ascribed status**, assigned at birth without regard to one\'s choices or efforts. Physician, college graduate and Olympic athlete are **achieved statuses**, earned through merit and effort.'},
  {ofTag:'Social Psychology & Society',of:'_____ focuses on how anomic conditions can lead to deviance, and in turn reinforce social stratification',kind:'scenario',q:'A young man embraces society\'s goal of financial success but lacks access to good schools or jobs, so he turns to selling stolen goods. Which theory most directly explains his deviance?',choices:['Strain theory','Social control theory','Labeling theory','Differential association theory'],ans:0,exp:'Merton\'s **strain theory** proposes that deviance arises when people accept culturally approved **goals** but are blocked from legitimate **means** of reaching them; this response is called innovation. Differential association would instead attribute the behavior to learning from deviant peers.'},
  {ofTag:'Social Psychology & Society',of:'_____ has serious consequences: a severe negative social reaction produces a stigmatizing label, which leads to more deviant behavior',kind:'scenario',q:'After being arrested for vandalism, a teenager is labeled a "delinquent" by teachers and neighbors. He begins to see himself that way and commits more serious crimes. This later behavior is an example of:',choices:['primary deviance','secondary deviance','anomie','differential association'],ans:1,exp:'**Secondary deviance** follows when a stigmatizing **label is internalized** and leads to further deviance, as described by labeling theory. The original act of vandalism, before the label took hold, was primary deviance.'},
  {ofTag:'Social Psychology & Society',of:'_____ are informal norms that are less significant but still shape our everyday behavior',kind:'scenario',q:'A job applicant arrives at a formal interview wearing pajamas. Others find it odd, but there are no legal or serious moral consequences. His behavior violates which kind of norm?',choices:['Laws','Mores','Folkways','Taboos'],ans:2,exp:'**Folkways** are informal norms of everyday conduct, such as appropriate dress, whose violation brings only mild sanctions like disapproval or ridicule. Mores carry moral significance and severe sanctions, and taboos provoke revulsion.'},
  {ofTag:'Social Psychology & Society',of:'_____ is the condition in which people lack the minimum income needed to maintain the average standard of living in the society in which they live',kind:'scenario',q:'A U.S. family has adequate food and housing but cannot afford a car, internet service, or the school trips most classmates take. Sociologists would describe this family as experiencing:',choices:['social exclusion','downward mobility','absolute poverty','relative poverty'],ans:3,exp:'**Relative poverty** is defined against the **typical standard of living** in one\'s own society. This family meets its basic needs, so it is not in absolute poverty, which means lacking subsistence necessities such as food and shelter.'},
  {ofTag:'Social Psychology & Society',of:'_____ is the total number of existing cases of a disease per population at a given time',kind:'prediction',q:'A new drug greatly prolongs survival in a chronic disease but neither cures it nor prevents new cases. Over the next decade, what will most likely happen to the disease\'s incidence and prevalence?',choices:['Incidence is unchanged; prevalence increases','Incidence increases; prevalence is unchanged','Incidence is unchanged; prevalence decreases','Incidence decreases; prevalence decreases'],ans:0,exp:'**Prevalence** counts all existing cases, so patients living longer with the disease accumulate and prevalence rises; **incidence** counts only new cases, which the drug does not prevent. A cure, or faster death, would lower prevalence instead.'},
  {ofTag:'Social Psychology & Society',of:'_____ is the practice of judging other cultures based on the values and beliefs of one\'s own culture',kind:'scenario',q:'A tourist calls a host country\'s traditional dishes "disgusting" and its customs "backward" because they differ from those of his home country. Which concept does this illustrate?',choices:['Cultural relativism','Ethnocentrism','Xenocentrism','Cultural diffusion'],ans:1,exp:'Judging another culture by the standards of **one\'s own** culture is **ethnocentrism**. Cultural relativism is the opposite stance, understanding practices within their own cultural context, and xenocentrism is preferring another culture over one\'s own.'},
  {ofTag:'Personality, Development & Disorders',of:'_____ is the degree to which people believe they control the outcomes of events in their lives, as opposed to external forces',kind:'scenario',q:'A student consistently credits her good grades to luck and easy tests and blames her poor grades on unfair teachers. She most likely has:',choices:['a growth mindset','an internal locus of control','an external locus of control','high self-efficacy'],ans:2,exp:'Attributing outcomes to **forces outside oneself**, such as luck or other people, reflects an **external locus of control**. People with an internal locus attribute outcomes to their own effort and ability, which is associated with greater persistence.'},
  {ofTag:'Personality, Development & Disorders',of:'_____ is a mature defense mechanism in which socially unacceptable impulses are transformed into socially acceptable actions or behavior',kind:'scenario',q:'A teenager with intense aggressive impulses channels them into competitive boxing and eventually becomes a disciplined amateur champion. Which defense mechanism is this?',choices:['Reaction formation','Projection','Displacement','Sublimation'],ans:3,exp:'Transforming unacceptable impulses into **socially valued activities** is **sublimation**, considered the most mature defense mechanism. Displacement redirects the impulse itself onto a safer target, such as kicking a wall after an argument.'},
  {ofTag:'Personality, Development & Disorders',of:'_____ is a defense mechanism in which individuals attribute characteristics they find unacceptable in themselves to another person',kind:'scenario',q:'A man who is strongly attracted to a coworker repeatedly accuses his wife of flirting with other men. Which defense mechanism is he most likely using?',choices:['Projection','Displacement','Rationalization','Reaction formation'],ans:0,exp:'He attributes his **own unacceptable desires to someone else**, which is **projection**. Reaction formation would instead involve expressing the opposite of his feelings, such as acting unusually cold toward the coworker.'},
  {ofTag:'Social Psychology & Society',of:'The _____ states that three types of information influence our causal attributions: consensus, distinctiveness, and consistency',kind:'scenario',q:'Only Jake laughs at a particular comedian (low consensus), Jake laughs at nearly every comedian (low distinctiveness), and he laughs at this comedian every time (high consistency). According to the covariation model, observers will most likely attribute Jake\'s laughter to:',choices:['the comedian being exceptionally funny','something about Jake\'s disposition','the circumstances of that particular show','random chance'],ans:1,exp:'In Kelley\'s **covariation model**, low consensus, low distinctiveness and high consistency together point to an **internal (dispositional) attribution**: something about Jake. High consensus and high distinctiveness would instead point to the comedian, the stimulus.'},
];
